\cs19\b\f1\fs20\ul\super #{\footnote \pard\plain \s18\ql \li0\ri0\widctlpar\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0\itap0 \fs20\lang1033\langfe1033\cgrid\langnp1033\langfenp1033 {\cs19\super #}{ HIDD_DIALOG_REGION_NEW}}}{\b\f1\fs20\ul New Region:}{
\f1\fs20 
\par 
\par This dialogue box is used to create a new region to be added to an existing Dungeon Siege map.
\par 
\par }{\i\f1\fs20\ul Settings:}{\i\f1\fs20 
\par }\pard \ql \fi720\li0\ri0\nowidctlpar\faauto\rin0\lin0\itap0 {\i\f1\fs20 Select Map to Create Region ~
\par }\pard\plain \s15\ql \li720\ri0\nowidctlpar\faauto\rin0\lin720\itap0 \f1\fs20\lang1033\langfe1033\cgrid\langnp1033\langfenp1033 {
Use the drop down menu to select the map to which the new region will be added. If the intended map does not yet exist, it can be defined and created by clicking on the "New Map" button.
\par }\pard\plain \ql \li0\ri0\nowidctlpar\faauto\rin0\lin0\itap0 \fs24\lang1033\langfe1033\cgrid\langnp1033\langfenp1033 {\f1\fs20 
\par }{\i\f1\fs20\ul Region Settings:}{\i\f1\fs20 
\par }\pard \ql \fi-720\li720\ri0\nowidctlpar\faauto\rin0\lin720\itap0 {\i\f1\fs20 \tab Region Name ~
\par }{\f1\fs20 \tab This is the name of the map as it will appear in the directory structure.
\par }\pard \ql \li0\ri0\nowidctlpar\faauto\rin0\lin0\itap0 {\f1\fs20 
\par }\pard \ql \fi-720\li720\ri0\nowidctlpar\faauto\rin0\lin720\itap0 {\f1\fs20 \tab }{\i\f1\fs20 Scid Range ~
\par \tab }{\f1\fs20 This allows the user to enter the Scid (Static Content Identifier) number that the Siege Editor will begin with when placing node tiles.  Scid numbers are hexadecimal numbers that are unique to each no
de in the entire map and are used as reference by certain triggers, navigation, and identification. If no number is entered, the lowest unused number (within the map) will be used by default.
\par 
\par \tab }{\i\f1\fs20 Region ID ~ 
\par \tab }{\f1\fs20 This allows the user to enter a specific Region ID.  This hexadecimal number is unique for each region in a given map. If no number is entered, the lowest unused number (within the map) will be used by default.
\par 
\par }{\i\f1\fs20\ul Select Starting Terrain Node:}{\i\f1\fs20 
\par }\pard \ql \li0\ri0\nowidctlpar\faauto\rin0\lin0\itap0 {\f1\fs20 Using the directory tree, users can select which node will be 
the anchor point of the new region.  A starting node is required for every new map, and will, by default, contain a starting point.  The starting point can be altered or removed, and the starting node can be changed at a later time, if needed.
\par 
\par }{\i\f1\fs20\ul Related Editor Dialogue Windows:
\par }{\f1\fs20 Settings ~> Region
\par 
\par }{\i\f1\fs20\ul Related Siege University Courses:
\par }{\f1\fs20 200: Dungeon Siege Concepts and Terminology
\par \page }{\cs19\f1\fs20\super #{\footnote \pard\plain \s18\ql \li0\ri0\widctlpar\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0\itap0 \fs20\lang1033\langfe1033\cgrid\langnp1033\langfenp1033 {\cs19\super #}{ HIDD_DIALOG_NEW_MAP}}}{\b\f1\fs20\ul New Map:
\par 
\par }{\i\f1\fs20\ul Name:
\par }{\f1\fs20 This is the name of the map, as it will appear in the directory structure.
\par 
\par }{\i\f1\fs20\ul Screen Name:
\par }{\f1\fs20 This is the name of the map, as it will appear on the in-game map list.
\par 
\par }{\i\f1\fs20\ul Description:
\par }{\f1\fs20 This is the description text that will appear in the Map Description box when the map is selected from the list of available maps.}{\f1\fs20\ul 
\par 
\par }{\i\f1\fs20\ul Starting Time of Day:
\par }{\f1\fs20 Using the drop d
own menus, the user can set the 24-hour clock to indicate the in-game time of day represented when a new game begins on the map.  This is useful for lighting effects, which can be set to run on timers to simulate the passage of time from morning to noon t
o night.
\par 
\par }{\i\f1\fs20\ul World Frustum Radius:
\par }{\f1\fs20 Dungeon Siege uses a process by which information and parts of the world are constantly streamed in and out.  The frustum is a circle around a given character that represents the part of the map that the computer currently ha
s loaded in memory.  By adjusting the radius of this circle, the user can control the amount of information available to a character at any given time.  If no number is entered here, the radius measurement will default to 45 meters.
\par 
\par }{\i\f1\fs20\ul World Interest Radius:
\par }{\f1\fs20 
Each object can be assigned an interest radius that is measured in meters.  This number indicates how close a character must be to the node that an object is on for the object to be visible to the player.  Objects with no specified interest radius will de
fault to the world interest radius.  If the World Interest Radius is not specified, it will default to 4 meters.
\par 
\par }{\i\f1\fs20\ul Development-Only Map:
\par }{\f1\fs20 Selecting this check box will hide the map so that it does not show up on the in-game map list.
\par 
\par }{\i\f1\fs20\ul Related Editor Dialogue Windows:
\par }{\f1\fs20 Settings ~> Map
\par 
\par }{\i\f1\fs20\ul Related Siege University Courses:
\par }{\f1\fs20 200: Dungeon Siege Concepts and Terminology
\par \page }{\cs19\f1\fs20\super #{\footnote \pard\plain \s18\ql \li0\ri0\widctlpar\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0\itap0 \fs20\lang1033\langfe1033\cgrid\langnp1033\langfenp1033 {\cs19\super #}{ HIDD_DIALOG_BATCH_LNC_GENERATION}}}{\b\f1\fs20\ul 
Batch LNC Generation:
\par 
\par }{\f1\fs20 This tool is used for region recalculation.  Recalculating is a means by which the .LNC fil
es used in Dungeon Siege are deleted and then recreated.  This is done anytime changes are made outside of the Siege Editor that would affect the given region.  For example, a node mesh is changed, or a new node is created and inserted in place of a previ
ous node, or .gas files are manually edited.
\par 
\par }{\i\f1\fs20\ul Select Map:
\par }{\f1\fs20 Using the directory tree, multiple regions and entire maps can be selected for recalculating.
\par 
\par }{\i\f1\fs20\ul Full Region Recalculation:
\par }{\f1\fs20 When this option is checked, all sections of the .LNC files are recalculated.
\par 
\par }{\i\f1\fs20\ul Partial Recalculation:
\par }{\f1\fs20 The checkboxes in this section allow specific areas to be recalculated
\par 
\par }{\i\f1\fs20\ul Full Save:
\par }{\f1\fs20 This is a legacy option that is no longer used.  This option will begin a regular save without recalculating the region.
\par \page }{\cs19\f1\fs20\super #{\footnote \pard\plain \s18\ql \li0\ri0\widctlpar\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0\itap0 \fs20\lang1033\langfe1033\cgrid\langnp1033\langfenp1033 {\cs19\super #}{ HIDD_DIALOG_EXTRACT_TANK}}}{\b\f1\fs20\ul 
Convert .dsmap to Files
\par 
\par }{\f1\fs20 In order to edit a region in Siege Editor, it must be in an uncompressed format.  This command can be used to "rip" .dsmap files into their uncompressed state.  
\par 
\par }{\f1\fs20\ul Source File:
\par }{\f1\fs20 Use the "..." button to browse to the .dsmap file you wish to uncompress.
\par 
\par }{\f1\fs20\ul Destination File:
\par }{\f1\fs20 Use the "..." button to browse to the folder where you wish to place the uncompressed version of the files.
\par 
\par }{\f1\fs20\ul Use default destination folder:
\par }{\f1\fs20 Selecting this option will gray out the destination folder path, disabling the option to browse and, thus, setting the currently selected destination folder as the default.
\par 
\par }{\f1\fs20\ul Extract .lqd files:
\par }{\f1\fs20 Dungeon Siege compresses .gas files into more streamlined .lqd files.  Checking this option will allow the user to extract the .gas files
 back out.  Though this functionality is present, this option is rarely used since it is just as easy to simply edit the .gas file(s) and allow the game to recompress the .lqd files automatically.
\par \page }{\cs19\f1\fs20\super #{\footnote \pard\plain \s18\ql \li0\ri0\widctlpar\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0\itap0 \fs20\lang1033\langfe1033\cgrid\langnp1033\langfenp1033 {\cs19\super #}{ HIDD_DIALOG_SAVE_AS_DSMOD}}}{\b\f1\fs20\ul 
Save folder as .dsmod:
\par 
\par }{\f1\fs20 
This dialogue is rarely used and it is suggested that only advanced users attempt to make changes to the default settings. It is further suggested that users simply use the "Save files as .dsmap" function under the File Menu to save new maps, changes, and
 modifications.
\par 
\par However, if you need to alter the actual compression process, that can be done here.
\par 
\par }{\i\f1\fs20\ul Field Entries:
\par }{\f1\fs20 The data fields allow for editing of default source folders, destination folders, output prefix (directory path), map title, author, copyright information, version number, and a brief description.
\par 
\par }{\i\f1\fs20\ul LQD Files:
\par }{\f1\fs20 Check boxes in this section will determine if .LQD files are automatically generated, if they are included in the compression to .dsmap or if they are simply deleted.  .LQD files that Du
ngeon Siege uses and are the compressed, more efficient versions  of .GAS files.
\par 
\par }{\i\f1\fs20\ul Priority:
\par }{\f1\fs20 Allows the user to determine the heirarchy settings for multiple tanks (builds).  If multiple tanks contain the same file, this setting is used to denote which tank is used.
\par 
\par }{\i\f1\fs20\ul Checkboxes:
\par }{\f1\fs20 The checkboxes allow the user to decide which compression/backup processes are used, set privacy, or determine if the map will show up on the in-game map list.
\par \page }{\cs19\f1\fs20\super #{\footnote \pard\plain \s18\ql \li0\ri0\widctlpar\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0\itap0 \fs20\lang1033\langfe1033\cgrid\langnp1033\langfenp1033 {\cs19\super #}{ HIDD_DIALOG_SAVE_AS_DSMAP}}}{\b\f1\fs20\ul 
Save Map as .dsmap:
\par 
\par }{\f1\fs20 Before a map can be pl
ayed in-game, it must be saved into a compiled .dsmap format.  From the Source Map window in this dialogue box, you can select which map you wish to compile, and then use the "..." button to browse to the directory where you wish to save the .dsmap.

\par \page }{\cs19\f1\fs20\super #{\footnote \pard\plain \s18\ql \li0\ri0\widctlpar\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0\itap0 \fs20\lang1033\langfe1033\cgrid\langnp1033\langfenp1033 {\cs19\super #}{ HIDD_DIALOG_REGION_LOAD}}}{\b\f1\fs20\ul Open:

\par 
\par }{\f1\fs20 Using the directory tree displayed, the user can select the region to be opened for editing.  In order to create a new region, go to the "New Region" listing in the File Menu.
\par 
\par }{\i\f1\fs20\ul Full Region Recalculation:
\par }\pard\plain \s20\ql \li0\ri0\nowidctlpar\faauto\rin0\lin0\itap0 \f1\fs20\lang1033\langfe1033\cgrid\langnp1033\langfenp1033 {
This function is used when corruption problems occur in the .LNC file.  By checking this box, the LNC file for the selected region will be deleted and rebuilt using the .GAS file information.
\par }\pard\plain \ql \li0\ri0\nowidctlpar\faauto\rin0\lin0\itap0 \fs24\lang1033\langfe1033\cgrid\langnp1033\langfenp1033 {\i\f1\fs20\ul 
\par Tuning Load:
\par }{\f1\fs20 Checking this box will load the selected region and supply \'93pcontent\'94
 items.  Pcontent is the system by which items within a given range are supplied.  This is how monsters, NPCs, and containers can contain/possess different specific items each time the game is played.  Loading with this option can offer 
help with tuning and balancing issues.  It is important that you DO NOT SAVE when a region is loaded with this option checked.  If you save a tuning load, characters, monsters, and containers will be saved with the specific items they loaded as opposed to
 being saved with a variable pcontent value.
\par 
\par }{\i\f1\fs20\ul Related Siege University Courses:
\par }{\f1\fs20 305: Parameterized Content (Pcontent)
\par \page }{\cs19\f1\fs20\super #{\footnote \pard\plain \s18\ql \li0\ri0\widctlpar\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0\itap0 \fs20\lang1033\langfe1033\cgrid\langnp1033\langfenp1033 {\cs19\super #}{ HIDD_DIALOG_SAVE_REGION}}}{\b\f1\fs20\ul Save:}{
\f1\fs20 
\par 
\par This function will save any changes made during the current editing session.
\par 
\par NOTE - This procedure will save the individual files, the map will still need to be converted to a .dsmap to be playable in Dungeon Siege.
\par }\pard \ql \li0\ri0\widctlpar\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0\itap0 {\f1\fs20 \page }{\cs19\f1\fs20\super #{\footnote \pard\plain \s18\ql \li0\ri0\widctlpar\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0\itap0 
\fs20\lang1033\langfe1033\cgrid\langnp1033\langfenp1033 {\cs19\super #}{ HIDD_DIALOG_KEYBOARD_SETTINGS}}}{\b\fs20\ul Keyboard Shortcuts:
\par 
\par }{\fs20 This tool allows the user to assign hotkeys to commonly used functions.
\par }{\b\fs20\ul 
\par }{\i\fs20\ul Commands:
\par }{\fs20 This window displays the functions to which hotkeys can be assigned.  Selecting the desired function and then using the \'93Key Mapping\'94 drop down list to the right assigns the desired hotkey.
\par 
\par }{\i\fs20\ul Clear Key:
\par }{\fs20 When a function is selected from the \'93Commands\'94 window and this button is clicked, any existing hotkey associated with that function will be removed.
\par 
\par }{\i\fs20\ul Reset All:
\par }{\fs20 When this button is used, all hotkey associations will be removed.
\par 
\par }{\i\fs20\ul Key Mapping:
\par }{\fs20 Once a function is selected from the \'93Commands\'94
 window, the desired hotkey can then be selected from this drop down list.  Additionally, the Alt, Control, and Shift, selections can be used in addition to the actual key assigned in the drop down list.
\par 
\par }{\i\fs20\ul Assign Key:
\par }{\fs20 Once a function is selected, and the desired hotkey to be associated is selected, clicking the \'93Assign Key\'94 will finalize the process and link the hotkey to the function.
\par }{\f1\fs20 \page }{\cs19\f1\fs20\super #{\footnote \pard\plain \s18\ql \li0\ri0\widctlpar\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0\itap0 \fs20\lang1033\langfe1033\cgrid\langnp1033\langfenp1033 {\cs19\super #}{ HIDD_DIALOG_HUDS}}}{\b\fs20\ul Debug HUDs

\par 
\par }{\fs20 Heads Up Displays (HUDs) are graphic representations of information contain within and acte
d upon by various groups of objects.  For instance, an actor's HUD displays a series of concentric circles indicating his weapon range, engagement range, and communication range, plus other information.  This dialogue window is used to determine which HUD
s will be visible and which groups of will groups will display them.  
\par 
\par }{\i\fs20\ul Group:
\par }{\fs20 By selecting from the options available, the user can determine which group will display HUDs - Actors, Items, Commands, or Miscellaneous.  Additionally, the user can select that
 "All" or "None" of them to display HUDs.  Only one of the groups can be selected at a time, with the exception of the "All" selection, which will enable all of the groups' HUDs.
\par 
\par }{\i\fs20\ul Option:
\par }{\fs20 By selecting from the options available, the user can determine whic
h types of HUDs will be displayed. Additionally, if the "Labels" option is enabled, the various parts of the HUDs displayed will have text labels indicating the purpose of the various parts of the given HUD.
\par 
\par NOTE ~ Not all of the Groups display all of the Options.
\par 
\par }{\i\fs20\ul Single Object:
\par }{\fs20 When enabled, the user can select individual members of each of the Groups to display a HUD.
\par 
\par NOTE ~ Object selection must be enabled. Also, the Single Object setting works best if the Group setting is set to "None".
\par 
\par }{\i\fs20\ul Related Editor Dialogue Windows:
\par }{\fs20 View ~> Preferences
\par 
\par }{\i\fs20\ul Related Siege University Courses:
\par }{\fs20 200: Dungeon Siege Concepts and Terminology
\par }{\f1\fs20 \page }{\cs19\f1\fs20\super #{\footnote \pard\plain \s18\ql \li0\ri0\widctlpar\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0\itap0 \fs20\lang1033\langfe1033\cgrid\langnp1033\langfenp1033 {\cs19\super #}{ HIDD_DIALOG_DECAL_PREFERENCES}}}{
\b\fs20\ul Decal Preferences:
\par 
\par }{\fs20 The data fields allow the user to set the default horizontal and verticle dimensions for decals.
\par 
\par }{\i\fs20\ul Related Siege University Courses:
\par }{\fs20 200: Dungeon Siege Concepts and Terminology
\par }{\f1\fs20 \page }{\cs19\f1\fs20\super #{\footnote \pard\plain \s18\ql \li0\ri0\widctlpar\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0\itap0 \fs20\lang1033\langfe1033\cgrid\langnp1033\langfenp1033 {\cs19\super #}{ HIDD_DIALOG_SELECTION_FILTERS}}}{
\b\fs20\ul Selection Filters:
\par 
\par }{\fs20 By selecting the checkboxe
s in this dialogue window, the user can filter out the ability to click on and select certain types of items.  This is prevents accidentally selecting a particular item. It is a particularly useful tool when editing areas with a large number of items, whe
re selecting a specific item can be difficult due to the number of items around it.
\par 
\par }{\i\fs20\ul Don't Select:
\par }{\fs20 Checking any box will filter out the ability to click on and select that item type in the editor main window. }{\i\fs20\ul 
\par 
\par Select All Override:
\par }{\fs20 When checked, this option overrides the settings above it and makes all of the items listed selectable.
\par 
\par NOTE ~ even if filtered, items can be selected via click-and-drag methods.  The filter only disallows selection by clicking on the item type.
\par 
\par }{\i\fs20\ul Related Editor Dialogue Windows:
\par }{\fs20 View ~> Preferences
\par 
\par }{\i\fs20\ul Related Siege University Courses:
\par }{\fs20 200: Dungeon Siege Concepts and Terminology
\par }{\f1\fs20 \page }{\cs19\f1\fs20\super #{\footnote \pard\plain \s18\ql \li0\ri0\widctlpar\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0\itap0 \fs20\lang1033\langfe1033\cgrid\langnp1033\langfenp1033 {\cs19\super #}{ HIDD_DIALOG_PREFERENCES}}}{\b\fs20\ul 
Preferences:
\par 
\par }{\fs20 This dialogue window is used to set preferences which will affect how the working region will be displayed in Siege Editor.
\par }\pard \ql \fi720\li0\ri0\widctlpar\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0\itap0 {\fs20 
\par }\pard \ql \li0\ri0\widctlpar\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0\itap0 {\i\fs20\ul Node Settings:
\par }{\i\fs20 \tab Wireframe ~
\par \tab }{\fs20 This will display all elements in a wire frame form.
\par 
\par \tab }{\i\fs20 Door Draw ~
\par }\pard \ql \fi-720\li720\ri0\widctlpar\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin720\itap0 {\i\fs20 \tab }{\fs20 When enabled, all door sections will be drawn, showing the in
dividual nodes in the working region.  Connected doors will show in green, while unconnected doors will be red.  This is a useful tool for verification that nodes are correctly placed.
\par 
\par \tab }{\i\fs20 Floor Drawing ~
\par \tab }{\fs20 When enabled, all walkable surfaces in the region will be highlighted in white. 
\par 
\par \tab }{\i\fs20 Water Draw ~
\par \tab }{\fs20 Similar to "Floor Drawing", this option will highlight all water areas in white.
\par 
\par \tab }{\i\fs20 Box Drawing ~
\par \tab }{\fs20 When enabled, a small box appears on the tip of the pointer.  When moved around, and the small box will show "collisions" with other object boxes and the other object box will appear.  This is a useful tool for testing hit detections.
\par 
\par \tab }{\i\fs20 Draw Door Numbers ~
\par \tab }{\fs20 When enabled, selected nodes will also display door numbers.
\par 
\par \tab }{\i\fs20 Draw Logical Nodes ~
\par \tab }{\fs20 This option will break the
 region up into its logical nodes.  Logical nodes show which parts of a node are walkable and which parts are unwalkable.  Walkable logical nodes are displayed in blue.  Blocking or unwalkable nodes are displayed in red.
\par 
\par \tab }{\i\fs20 Draw Stitch Doors ~
\par \tab }{\fs20 When enabled, doors that are stitched to other regions will be highlighted with yellow.
\par 
\par \tab }{\i\fs20 Hit Detect Floors Only ~
\par \tab }{\fs20 When enabled, objects can only be placed on floored (walkable) areas.
\par 
\par }{\i\fs20\ul Rendering:
\par }{\i\fs20 \tab Dynamic Lighting ~
\par \tab }{\fs20 This option toggles whether or not the effects of dynamic lighting are visible in the editor.  (i.e. - torches, flames, etc.) 
\par }{\i\fs20 
\par \tab Static Lighting ~
\par \tab }{\fs20 This option toggles whether or not the effects of static lighting are visible in the editor.  (i.e. - spotlights, pointlights, etc.)
\par }{\i\fs20 
\par \tab Dynamic Shadows ~
\par \tab }{\fs20 This option toggles whether or not dynamic objects cast shadows while in the editor.
\par \tab (i.e. - actors, monsters, etc)
\par }{\i\fs20 
\par \tab Static Shadows ~
\par \tab }{\fs20 This option toggles whether or not static objects cast shadows while in the editor.
\par \tab (i.e. - houses, rocks, etc)
\par 
\par }{\i\fs20 \tab Dynamic Shading ~
\par \tab }{\fs20 This option toggles whether or not dynamic objects are affected by light sources while in the editor.
\par }{\i\fs20 
\par \tab Static Shading ~
\par \tab }{\fs20 This option toggles whether or not static objects are affected by light sources while in the editor.
\par }{\i\fs20 
\par \tab Ignore Vertex Lighting ~ }{\fs20 When enabled, all terrain and objects are shown fully lit, regardless of any light settings.  When disabled, you will see the re
gion as it would appear with the current light settings for ambient, directional, point, and spot lights.
\par 
\par \tab }{\i\fs20 Show Polygon Statistics ~
\par \tab }{\fs20 This tool is used to see how heavily weighted a given area is with artwork. When enabled, a series of numbers appear in the upper left corner of the editor\rquote 
s Main Window.  The table of information is divided into four rows and four columns.  The rows are labeled T, V, ST, SV and show the number of triangles, vertices, selected triangles and selected vertices, respectively.  
In the case of triangles and vertices, the numbers refer to the number of each appearing in the entire field of vision of the editor\rquote 
s Main Window.  The selected triangles and vertices entries show the same information for the individual object that is cur
rently under the cursor. The information is further divided into the four columns, which represent the T, V, ST, and SV counts for terrain, objects, and nodes.  The fourth column, which only appears for the full field of vision, shows grand totals. 

\par \tab }{\i\fs20 
\par \tab Draw Normals ~
\par \tab }{\fs20 When enabled, normals 
will be indicated on polygons.  Normals are the angle perpendicular to the plane of a polygon.  This is important, because the direction of the normal, when taken into account in conjunction with the direction of a light source, determines how the light w
ill reflect off the polygon and the effect the intensity and color of the light will have on the polygon.}{\i\fs20 
\par 
\par \tab Draw Commands ~
\par \tab }{\fs20 This option will toggle whether or not commands are visible while in the editor.
\par }{\i\fs20 
\par }{\i\fs20\ul Misc.
\par }{\i\fs20 \tab Update World ~
\par \tab }{\fs20 When enabled, all factors o
f the world will continually update.  Timed lights will follow timing adjustments, creatures will follow programmed commands and fidgets, effects will run, etc.  In short, it the region will look exactly like it will in game.  NOTE ~ DO NOT SAVE if "Updat
e World" has been activated at any time in the editing session.  The data saved will reflect the latest settings and positions of the world state, as opposed to the actual starting states originally set up.}{\i\fs20 
\par 
\par \tab Update Weather ~
\par \tab }{\fs20 When enabled, weather and mood settings will update consistently in accordance with their settings.}{\i\fs20 
\par \tab 
\par \tab Disable Rendering Settings ~
\par \tab }{\fs20 When enabled, all of the Node Settings and Rendering options, as well as several of the Misc. Options will be grayed out and inaccessible.}{\i\fs20 
\par 
\par \tab FPS Data ~
\par \tab }{\fs20 Framerate data (Frames Per Second) will be shown in the upper left corner of the main window.  NOTE ~ the FPS data relates to the Siege Editor, NOT to the FPS that would persist in-game.}{\i\fs20 
\par 
\par \tab Update Lighting/Effects ~
\par \tab }{\fs20 When enabled, lighting and lighting effe
cts will update.  NOTE ~ DO NOT SAVE if "Update Lighting/Effects" has been activated at any time in the editing session.  The data saved will reflect the latest settings and positions of the lighting, as opposed to the actual starting states originally se
t up.}{\i\fs20 
\par 
\par \tab Right Click Camera Scrolling ~
\par \tab }{\fs20 When enabled, clicking and holding the right mouse button will allow for movement around the map.  When disabled, right clicking will allow access to properties for the selected node or item, as well as other options 
such as cut, copy, paste, and hide.  (Options available will be dependant on the type of object selected.)}{\i\fs20 
\par 
\par \tab Update Sound ~
\par \tab }{\fs20 When enabled, the sound associated with the region will update.}{\i\fs20 
\par 
\par \tab Lock Custom View ~
\par \tab }{\fs20 This is a legacy item and is no longer used or functional.
\par }{\i\fs20 
\par \tab Draw Logical Flags ~
\par \tab }{\fs20 When enabled, areas associated with logical flags will be highlighted in blue.  Logical flags are used to determine if an area is walkable or not, and what types of creatures or characters can walk there.}{\i\fs20 
\par 
\par \tab Draw Light Data ~
\par \tab }{\fs20 When enabled, this will draw the inner and outer light radius of selected light sources.
\par 
\par }{\i\fs20\ul Content Display:
\par }\pard \ql \li0\ri0\widctlpar\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0\itap0 {\fs20 This drop down menu allows the user to determine how the directory tree is shown in the File Win
dow, either by its Development Name or its Screen Name.  It is suggested that the user leave the setting at its default Development Name setting, as not every item has a screen name.
\par }{\i\fs20\ul 
\par }\pard \ql \fi-720\li720\ri0\widctlpar\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin720\itap0 {\i\fs20\ul Key Movement Delta:
\par }\pard \ql \li0\ri0\widctlpar\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0\itap0 {\fs20 Altering this number will affect how quickly the the screen scrolls.  Raising the delta will create a faster scroll rate.
\par }{\i\fs20\ul 
\par }\pard \ql \fi-720\li720\ri0\widctlpar\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin720\itap0 {\i\fs20\ul Zoom Rate:
\par }\pard \ql \li0\ri0\widctlpar\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0\itap0 {\fs20 
Altering this number will affect the amount of "zoom" associated with each step.  Raising the number will create a faster zoom with large steps.  Lowering the number will cre
ate a slow zoom with small intervals, allowing for more a finer ability to adjust.}{\i\fs20\ul 
\par }{\fs20 
\par }{\i\fs20\ul Set Clear Color:
\par }{\fs20 This button will call up a color palette, from which the user can define the background color (default: black).  The user can either pick the color from the palette, or enter RGB values.
\par 
\par }{\i\fs20\ul Related Editor Dialogue Windows:
\par }{\fs20 View ~> Camera Controller
\par Lighting ~> Ambient Lighting
\par Lighting ~> Draw Directional Lights
\par }\pard\plain \s18\ql \li0\ri0\widctlpar\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0\itap0 \fs20\lang1033\langfe1033\cgrid\langnp1033\langfenp1033 {Node ~> Logical Flag Properties
\par }\pard\plain \ql \li0\ri0\widctlpar\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0\itap0 \fs24\lang1033\langfe1033\cgrid\langnp1033\langfenp1033 {\fs20 Object ~> Movement Locking
\par Object ~> Mode
\par 
