\par }{\f1\fs20 \page }{\cs19\f1\fs20\super #{\footnote \pard\plain \s18\ql \li0\ri0\widctlpar\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0\itap0 \fs20\lang1033\langfe1033\cgrid\langnp1033\langfenp1033 {\cs19\super #}{ node_properties}}}{\b\fs20\ul 
Node Properties:
\par 
\par }{\i\fs20\ul General:
\par }\pard \ql \li720\ri0\widctlpar\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin720\itap0 {\i\fs20 Node GUID ~
\par }{\fs20 This field displays the selected node's GUID (Globally Unique Identifier).  Every node in the entire map has a unique hexidecimal number that distinguishes it.  This GUID can be used as a reference for locating a node, and by many of the triggers.

\par 
\par }{\i\fs20 Mesh GUID ~
\par }{\fs20 Like the Node GUID, this hexidecimal number is used unique and is used to identify the mesh used by the selected node.
\par 
\par }{\i\fs20 SNO Filename ~
\par }{\fs20 This is the name of the node as it appears in the directory tree in File Window of the Siege Editor.
\par 
\par }{\i\fs20 Checkboxes ~
\par }\pard \ql \fi-720\li1440\ri0\widctlpar\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin1440\itap0 {\i\fs20 \tab }{\fs20 Occludes Light ~
\par \tab When selected, light will be blocked by terrain on this node.
\par 
\par \tab Hide Node ~
\par \tab When selected, the node will be hidden from view.
\par 
\par \tab Occludes Camera ~
\par \tab When selected, if the node blocks the camera at any point, the camera will auto-adjust to see around the node. 
\par 
\par \tab Bounds Camera ~
\par \tab When selected, the camera will not be able to clip (pass through) the node.
\par 
\par \tab Camera Fade ~
\par \tab When selected, if the node blocks the camera at any point, the node will fade to allow the camera to see through.
\par 
\par \tab Lock Lighting Calcs ~
\par }\pard \ql \li1440\ri0\widctlpar\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin1440\itap0 {\fs20 When selected, the node\rquote s current lighting effects will be locked in place.  This is used for tuning lights near a region border.
\par 
\par }\pard \ql \li90\ri0\widctlpar\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin90\itap0 {\i\fs20\ul Fade Settings:
\par }{\fs20 The data fields here allow the user to set values for Section, Level, and Object.  With these settings, nodes can b
e divided into these three category settings for the purposes of fading large groups of nodes.  For example, a user could set a trigger so that, when a character decends the stairs into a dungeon, all of the nodes for the world at the top of the stairs (f
o
r example, all nodes with a level setting of 1) will fade out, and all of the nodes for this particular dungeon (for example, all nodes in level 2, group 7) will fade in.  The three categories can be used individually or in any combination.  By default, t
he settings are -1.
\par 
\par }{\i\fs20\ul Node Set:
\par }{\fs20 This displays the current node set and texture being used by the selected node.  By using the drop down menu, the user can change the node set to gain access to other available textures.  NOTE ~ Changing the node set here will
 only change the setting for the selected node, as opposed to using the "Current Node Set" setting found in the Node Menu, which will change the selected node and remain in place for subsequent nodes.
\par 
\par }{\i\fs20\ul Stitching:
\par }{\fs20 When a door that is stitched to another region is selected, these data fields display the unique identifying number for the stitch and the region to which it is stitched.
\par 
\par }{\i\fs20\ul Related Editor Dialogue Windows:}{\fs20 
\par Node ~> Current Node Set
\par Lighting ~> Ambient Lighting
\par 
