\par }\pard \ql \li0\ri0\widctlpar\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0\itap0 {\f1\fs20 \page }{\cs19\f1\fs20\super #{\footnote \pard\plain \s18\ql \li0\ri0\widctlpar\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0\itap0 
\fs20\lang1033\langfe1033\cgrid\langnp1033\langfenp1033 {\cs19\super #}{ HIDD_DIALOG_LOGICALNODE_PROPERTIES}}}{\b\fs20\ul Logical Node Properties:
\par 
\par }{\fs20 Logical nodes are the pieces of nodes that are flagged as walkable or nonwalkable and further defined by indicating which template types can walk on the walkable sections.
\par 
\par }{\i\fs20\ul Logical Flags:
\par }{\fs20 This window displays a list of which flags actors may possess and be able to walk on the logical node.
\par 
\par }{\i\fs20\ul Available Flags:
\par }{\fs20 This drop down list provides the user a means of selecting which template flags can walk on the node.  The user adds flags to the Logical Flags window by selecting a flag in the drop down list, and then clicking on the "Add Flag" button.  Al
l of the flags begin with "lf_" (logical flag) and then indicate the creature type. (i.e. - lf_human_player, lf_boss, lf_dirt, etc.).   NOTE ~ at this time, it is not possible for users to define new logical flags other than those listed.  However, by edi
ting the individual creatures' templates, the user can assign which flags affect which creatures.
\par 
\par }{\i\fs20\ul Remove Flags:
\par }{\fs20 Selecting a flag from the Logical Flags window and clicking the \'93Remove Flag\'94 button will remove the selected flag.
\par 
\par }{\i\fs20\ul Clear Flags:
\par }{\fs20 When clicked, the "Clear Flags" button will remove all flags from the Logical Flags window, thus making the logical node unwalkable.
\par 
\par }{\i\fs20\ul Related Siege University Courses:
\par }{\fs20 101: Nodework
\par 106: Monster Placement
\par 207: Non-Interactive Sequences (NIS)
\par 304: Scripted Sequences
\par }{\f1\fs20 \page }{\cs19\f1\fs20\super #{\footnote \pard\plain \s18\ql \li0\ri0\widctlpar\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0\itap0 \fs20\lang1033\langfe1033\cgrid\langnp1033\langfenp1033 {\cs19\super #}{ HIDD_DIALOG_NODE_SET}}}{\b\fs20\ul 
Current Node Set:
\par 
\par }{\fs20 This drop down menu shows the current set from which the selected node's texture is pulled.  It also allows the user to change the node set to access different textures (ie. - changing a grassy textured node to one that is snow covered.)  
\par 
\par NOTE ~ When a change is made to the current node set, that setting will carry over to subsequently placed nodes.
\par 
\par }{\i\fs20\ul Related Editor Dialogue Windows:
\par }{\fs20 Node ~> Node Properties
\par 
\par }{\i\fs20\ul Related Siege University Courses:
\par }{\fs20 101: Nodework
\par 200: Dungeon Siege Concepts and Terminology
\par 211: Siege Max
\par }{\f1\fs20 \page }{\cs19\f1\fs20\super #{\footnote \pard\plain \s18\ql \li0\ri0\widctlpar\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0\itap0 \fs20\lang1033\langfe1033\cgrid\langnp1033\langfenp1033 {\cs19\super #}{ HIDD_DIALOG_NODE_FADE_GROUPS}}}{
\b\fs20\ul Node Fade Groups:
\par 
\par }{\fs20 This dialogue window is used to group sets of nodes together using their Section, Level, and Object values, and then hide or show them by group.  This is most useful when working on a region with multiple levels.
\par 
\par }{\i\fs20\ul Properties:
\par }{\fs20 By using the check boxes to enable the corresponding fields, and then entering values fo Section, Level, and/or Object, the user defines which nodes are included in the group to be defi
ned.  Any combination of the three properties can be used to define the group.  Clicking the "Add ->" button will then transfer the entered values into the Fade Settings display section.  It is possible to assign multiple settings to a single group.  Sele
cting a group of settings in the Fade Settings Menu and then clicking "<- Remove Selection" will remove that setting from the group.
\par 
\par NOTE ~ a group must be created and selected in the "Groups" drop down menu (see below) before entered data can be assigned.
\par 
\par }{\i\fs20\ul Fade Settings:
\par }{\fs20 This window shows the settings entered and added from the Properties section of the dialogue window.
\par 
\par }{\i\fs20\ul New Group:
\par }{\fs20 Clicking this button will create a new fade group and bring up another window for the user to name the new group.  After creating a new group and naming it, it will be selectable in the "Groups" pull-down menu.
\par 
\par }{\i\fs20\ul Delete Group:
\par }{\fs20 Clicking on the button will delete the selected Fade Group.
\par 
\par }{\i\fs20\ul Hide:}{\fs20 
\par Clicking on this button will hide all nodes matching the properties settings indicated by the selected Fade Group.}{\i\fs20\ul 
\par 
\par Show:
\par }{\fs20 Clicking on this button will "un-hide" all nodes matching the properties settings indicated by the selected Fade Group.}{\i\fs20\ul 
\par 
\par Select:
\par }{\fs20 Clicking this button will highlight the edges of all nodes matching the properties settings indicated by the selected Fade Group.  This is useful to see which nodes will be affected with the "Hide" or "Show" buttons.
\par }{\i\fs20\ul 
\par Groups:
\par }{\fs20 This drop-down menu is used to indicate which fade group is either being edited or used.
\par 
\par }{\i\fs20\ul Related Editor Dialogue Windows:
\par }{\fs20 Node ~> Node Properties
\par 
\par }{\i\fs20\ul Related Siege University Courses:
\par }{\fs20 101: Nodework
\par 104: Fading
\par 203A: Triggers I
\par 203B: Triggers II
\par 205: Fades}{
\par }{\f1\fs20 \page }{\cs19\f1\fs20\super #{\footnote \pard\plain \s18\ql \li0\ri0\widctlpar\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0\itap0 \fs20\lang1033\langfe1033\cgrid\langnp1033\langfenp1033 {\cs19\super #}{ HIDD_DIALOG_NODE_LIST}}}{\b\fs20\ul 
Node List:
\par }{\fs20 
\par This is a list of every Node GUID (Globally Unique Identifier) used in the region.  By selecting a GUID and then hitting the "Select" button, the Siege Editor will center on the indicated node.
\par 
\par }{\i\fs20\ul Related Editor Dialogue Windows:
\par }{\fs20 Node ~> Properties
\par Node ~> Node Fade Groups
\par Node ~> Mesh List
\par 
\par }{\i\fs20\ul Related Siege University Courses:
\par }{\fs20 101: Nodework
\par 104: Fading
\par 200: Dungeon Siege Concepts and Terminology
\par 203A: Triggers I
\par 203B: Triggers II
\par 211: Siege Max
\par }{\f1\fs20 \page }{\cs19\f1\fs20\super #{\footnote \pard\plain \s18\ql \li0\ri0\widctlpar\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0\itap0 \fs20\lang1033\langfe1033\cgrid\langnp1033\langfenp1033 {\cs19\super #}{ HIDD_DIALOG_MESH_LIST}}}{\b\fs20\ul 
Mesh List:
\par 
\par }{\fs20 The "Mesh List" functions similarly to the "Node Fade Groups" function in the Nodes Menu.  However, instead of defining groups by Level, Section, and Object values, the groups are instead created by lists of meshes. 
\par 
\par }{\i\fs20\ul Used Meshes:
\par }{\fs20 This window lists all of the meshes used in the current region.  The meshes are listed in order according to GUID, and also indicate the corresponding SNO files.  The SNO file is the name used in the directory tree in the File Window.
\par 
\par }{\i\fs20\ul Select Mesh:
\par }{\fs20 Selecting a mesh in the list and then clicking the "Select Mesh" button will highlight all instances of that mesh in the region.
\par 
\par }{\i\fs20\ul Add:
\par }{\fs20 When this button is clicked, the highlighted mesh in the list on the left side of the dialogue window will be added to th
e select fade group indicated on the right side.  NOTE ~ before meshes can be added to fade groups, the group must be created and selected in the "Select Group" drop down menu.
\par 
\par }{\i\fs20\ul Group:
\par }{\fs20 This window lists the meshes identified with the group selected in the "Select Group" drop down menu.
\par 
\par }{\i\fs20\ul New Group:
\par }{\fs20 This button will create a new group and bring up another window prompting the user to name the new group.  Once the group is created and named, it can be selected in the "Select Group" drop down menu.
\par 
\par }{\i\fs20\ul Delete Group:
\par }{\fs20 Clicking on this button will delete the group indicated in the "Select Group" drop down menu.
\par 
\par }{\i\fs20\ul Select Group:
\par }{\fs20 When this button is clicked, all nodes using the meshes indicated in the "Group" window will be highlighted on the map.
\par 
\par }{\i\fs20\ul Hide Group:
\par }{\fs20 When this button is clicked, all nodes using the meshes indicated in the "Group" window will be hidden.
\par 
\par }{\i\fs20\ul Show Group:
\par }{\fs20 When this button is clicked, all nodes using the meshes indicated in the "Group" window will reappear if hidden.
\par 
\par }{\i\fs20\ul Related Editor Dialogue Windows:
\par }{\fs20 Node ~> Properties
\par Node ~> Node Fade Groups
\par Node ~> Node List
\par 
\par }{\i\fs20\ul Related Siege University Courses:
\par }{\fs20 101: Nodework
\par 104: Fading
\par 200: Dungeon Siege Concepts and Terminology
\par 203A: Triggers I
\par 203B: Triggers II
\par 211: Siege Max
\par }{\f1\fs20 \page }{\cs19\f1\fs20\super #{\footnote \pard\plain \s18\ql \li0\ri0\widctlpar\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0\itap0 \fs20\lang1033\langfe1033\cgrid\langnp1033\langfenp1033 {\cs19\super #}{ HIDD_DIALOG_STITCH_MANAGER}}}{\b\fs20\ul 
Stitch Manager:
\par 
\par }{\fs20 Mainly a reference tool, the Stitch Manager lists information on all existing stitches in the region and throughout the map.
\par 
\par }{\i\fs20\ul Available Node IDs:
\par }{\fs20 \tab }{\i\fs20 Window ~
\par }\pard \ql \fi-720\li720\ri0\widctlpar\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin720\itap0 {\i\fs20 \tab }{\fs20 
This window displays a directory tree representation of all regions in the map an a list of all stitches in each region.  When any region folder is expanded, a list of each node in the re
gion that contain a stitch are listed.  Expanding any node will show the Node GUID, which door is stitched, and to which region it is stitched.
\par 
\par \tab }{\i\fs20 Open ~
\par \tab }{\fs20 Selecting a node in the directory tree above and then clicking on the "Open" button will open the node properties menu and display the properties corresponding to the highlighted node.
\par 
\par \tab }{\i\fs20 Remove ~
\par \tab }{\fs20 Highlighting a node in the directory tree above and clicking on "Remove" will delete the highlighted stitch.
\par 
\par \tab }{\i\fs20 Remove All ~
\par \tab }{\fs20 Highlighting a region in the directory tree above and clicking on the "Remove All" button will delete all stitches in the highlighted region.
\par 
\par }{\i\fs20\ul Region Stitch Information:
\par }\pard \ql \li0\ri0\widctlpar\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0\itap0 {\fs20 This window displays the number of stitches in the region highlighted in the directory tree above and to which regions the stitches correspond.
\par 
\par }{\i\fs20\ul Related Editor Dialogue Windows:
\par }{\fs20 View ~> Preferences
\par Node ~> Properties
\par Tools ~> Stitch Builder
\par Tools ~> Auto-Stitch Tool
\par 
\par }{\i\fs20\ul Related Siege University Courses:
\par }{\fs20 101: Nodework
\par 200: Dungeon Siege Concepts and Terminology
\par 206: Elevators
\par 307: Displacers
\par }{\f1\fs20 \page }{\cs19\f1\fs20\super #{\footnote \pard\plain \s18\ql \li0\ri0\widctlpar\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0\itap0 \fs20\lang1033\langfe1033\cgrid\langnp1033\langfenp1033 {\cs19\super #}{ object_properties}}}{\b\fs20\ul 
Object Properties:
\par 
\par }{\fs20 This dialogue window displays a large amount of information about the selected object.  The information is divided into several tabs and, in most cases, can be edited.
\par 
\par }{\i\fs20\ul Template Properties:
\par }{\fs20 \tab }{\i\fs20 Template Name ~
\par \tab }{\fs20 The name of the selected object as it appears in the directory tree.
\par 
\par \tab }{\i\fs20 Scid ~
\par \tab }{\fs20 The globally unique number used to identify the specific instance of the selected object.
\par 
\par \tab }{\i\fs20 Screen Name ~
\par \tab }{\fs20 The name of the object as it appears in-game.  Not all objects have screen names.
\par 
\par \tab }{\i\fs20 Hexadecimal / FourCC ~
\par }\pard \ql \li720\ri0\widctlpar\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin720\itap0 {\fs20 
Using these two options, the user can decide which format will be displayed - hexadecimal, or FourCC (Four Character Code).  Many of the data fields will show no difference between the two settings, but certai
n information (animations, for example) is much easier to read in its FourCC format.
\par }\pard \ql \li0\ri0\widctlpar\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0\itap0 {\fs20 
\par \tab }{\i\fs20 Component Fields ~
\par }\pard \ql \fi-720\li720\ri0\widctlpar\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin720\itap0 {\i\fs20 \tab }{\fs20 
The specific information listed in this window will vary depending on the object selected.  But in each case, a list of statistics and modifiers will
 be listed along with their current values, and a "Documentation" column that describes the purpose of each stat.  The values of the stats can be edited by clicking on the cell of the stat to be edited.
\par 
\par }{\i\fs20\ul Trigger Properties:
\par }{\fs20 \tab This tab is only used for triggers.  
\par 
\par \tab }{\i\fs20 Triggers ~
\par \tab }{\fs20 This window displays the trigger name
\par 
\par \tab }{\i\fs20 New / Remove ~
\par \tab }{\fs20 Using these buttons, the user can add or remove triggers in the "Triggers" window.
\par 
\par \tab }{\i\fs20 Trigger Properties ~
\par }\pard \ql \fi-1440\li1440\ri0\widctlpar\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin1440\itap0 {\i\fs20 \tab }{\fs20\ul One Shot:}{\i\fs20  }{\fs20 When selected, the trigger will only activate on the first instance of its conditions being met.
\par }\pard \ql \fi-720\li720\ri0\widctlpar\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin720\itap0 {\fs20 
\par \tab \tab }{\fs20\ul Single Player:}{\i\fs20  }{\fs20 When selected, the trigger will be active in single player games.
\par 
\par }\pard \ql \fi-1350\li1440\ri0\widctlpar\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin1440\itap0 {\fs20 \tab }{\fs20\ul Initially Active:}{\i\fs20  }{\fs20 When selected, the trigger will be active when it is first encountered.  Otherwise it 
will need to be activated by another trigger.}{\i\fs20 
\par }\pard \ql \fi-720\li720\ri0\widctlpar\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin720\itap0 {\i\fs20 
\par }\pard \ql \fi-720\li1440\ri0\widctlpar\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin1440\itap0 {\i\fs20 \tab }{\fs20\ul Flip-flop:}{\i\fs20  }{\fs20 
When selected, the trigger will have a action related to both when its conditions are met, and when its conditions are not met, similar to an IF / THEN / ELSE type of command.}{\i\fs20 
\par }\pard \ql \fi-720\li720\ri0\widctlpar\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin720\itap0 {\i\fs20 
\par \tab \tab }{\fs20\ul Multiplayer:}{\i\fs20  }{\fs20 When selected, the trigger will be active in multiplayer games.
\par 
\par \tab \tab }{\fs20\ul Can Self Destruct:
\par }\pard \ql \fi-720\li1440\ri0\widctlpar\tx1440\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin1440\itap0 {\i\fs20 \tab }{\fs20 When checked, the trigger will remove itself from the world once it has completed its tasking and is no longer needed.
\par }\pard \ql \fi-720\li720\ri0\widctlpar\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin720\itap0 {\fs20 
\par }\pard \ql \fi-1440\li1440\ri0\widctlpar\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin1440\itap0 {\i\fs20 \tab }{\fs20\ul Reset Duration:}{\fs20  The amount of time required to pass before the trigger can be "fired off" again.}{\i\fs20 
\par }\pard \ql \fi-720\li720\ri0\widctlpar\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin720\itap0 {\i\fs20 
\par \tab \tab }{\fs20\ul Delay:
\par }\pard \ql \fi-1440\li1440\ri0\widctlpar\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin1440\itap0 {\i\fs20 \tab }{\fs20 Entering a value here will delay, in seconds, the amoun
t of delay time added between when the trigger requirements are met and when it issues the resulting command(s).  Each individual action resulting from the trigger can also have }{\i\fs20 additional}{\fs20 
 delay time added within the properties of the actions in the actions window (see below).  In such a case, any delay time indicated for the action will be added to the triggers own delay time.
\par }\pard \ql \fi-720\li720\ri0\widctlpar\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin720\itap0 {\fs20 
\par }\pard \ql \fi-1440\li1440\ri0\widctlpar\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin1440\itap0 {\i\fs20 \tab }{\fs20\ul Occupants Group:}{\i\fs20  }{\fs20 
By entering a value here, the user can assign the trigger to a group of triggers with the same Occupants Group setting.  This setting can then be referenced to gather data from all triggers in the group.}{\i\fs20 
\par }\pard \qr \fi-720\li720\ri0\widctlpar\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin720\itap0 {\i\fs20 \tab \tab 
\par }\pard \ql \fi-720\li720\ri0\widctlpar\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin720\itap0 {\i\fs20 \tab Conditions ~
\par }\pard \ql \fi-1440\li1440\ri0\widctlpar\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin1440\itap0 {\i\fs20 \tab }{\fs20 
This window displays the conditions, which must be met for the trigger to force an action.  The name of the condition, its parameters, and the val
ues assigned to the parameters are displayed.  The name and parameters are static and cannot be changed.  But the values of the parameters can be altered.  To do so, double click on the cell of the value to be edited.  Depending on the type of data contai
ned in the cell, the user will be able to either edit directly or select a choice from a drop down menu.
\par 
\par \tab The user can add conditions to the trigger by clicking on the "Add Condition" button to the side.  This will bring up another dialogue window with a p
ull down menu, from which the user can select the desired condition.  The condition selected will determine what parameters are visible in the "Conditions" window.
\par 
\par \tab NOTE ~ for a trigger to perform its associated action, ALL conditions of the trigger must be met.
\par 
\par }\pard \ql \fi-720\li1440\ri0\widctlpar\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin1440\itap0 {\i\fs20 Actions ~
\par }\pard \ql \fi-1440\li1440\ri0\widctlpar\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin1440\itap0 {\i\fs20 \tab }{\fs20 
This window displays the actions, which will be performed when all conditions of the trigger are met.  The name of the action, its parameters, and the values assigned to the parameters are displayed, along with the delay of the action. 
 The name and parameters are static and cannot be changed.  But the values of the parameters can be altered.  To do so, double click on the cell of the value to be edited.  Depending on the type of data contained in the cell, the user will be able to eith
er edit directly or select a choice from a drop down menu.  The delay is the amount of time, in seconds, which the trigger will "wait" after its conditions are met and before the associated action is begun.
\par 
\par \tab The user can add actions to the trigger by click
ing on the "Add Action" button to the side.  This will bring up another dialogue window with a pull down menu, from which the user can select the desired condition.  The action selected will determine what parameters are visible in the "Actions" window.

\par 
\par }{\i\fs20\ul Rotation:
\par }\pard \ql \fi-720\li720\ri0\widctlpar\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin720\itap0 {\i\fs20 \tab }{\fs20 
By using the various inputs on this tab, the user can precisely define the orientation of the object in relation to its Pitch, Yaw, and Roll (X, Y, and Z axis, respectively).  In each case, the position is defined in degrees (0 - 359) around the 
associated axis.  The degree value can be entered in any of four ways. 
\par 
\par }\pard \ql \fi-1440\li1440\ri0\widctlpar\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin1440\itap0 {\fs20 \tab The user can use the dial settings by clicking and holding on the dial pointer and pulling the pointer around the dial.  
\par 
\par \tab The arrow keys under each dial can be used to adjust the o
bject along the given axis by a number of degrees equal to the number indicated in the "Rotation Snap Increment" field. In other words, if 45.0 is entered for the rotation snap increment, each time the arrow keys are used, the position will increase or de
crease around the selected axis by 45 degrees.
\par 
\par \tab The user can enter a value directly into the degree value field by double clicking on the field.
\par 
\par \tab The value can be set by using the drop down menu.
\par 
\par }{\i\fs20\ul Equipment:
\par }\pard \ql \fi-720\li720\ri0\widctlpar\tx720\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin720\itap0 {\fs20 \tab 
This tab and the information displayed on it are only of use to objects that are capable of being equipped.  In those cases, this tab is used to assign equipment to the selected object.
\par 
\par \tab }{\i\fs20 Equip Slot ~
\par \tab }{\fs20 By using the drop down menu, the user selects into which equipment slot (i.e. - head, ring, shield hand, etc.) the indicated item will go.
\par 
\par \tab }{\i\fs20 Selected Slot ~}{\fs20 
\par }\pard \ql \fi-720\li720\ri0\widctlpar\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin720\itap0 {\fs20 \tab 
By using the drop down menu, the user can set items by their associated Weapons Panel or spellbook slots (i.e. - melee / ranged weapon, spellbook slot 1-12).
\par 
\par \tab }{\i\fs20 Equipped ~
\par \tab }{\fs20 This area displays the template name, screen name, and documentation on the selected item.  It is a reference area and cannot be edited.
\par 
\par \tab }{\i\fs20 Available Items ~
\par \tab }{\fs20 This drop down menu allows the user to select, which item will be placed in th
e indicated "Equipment" or "Selected" slot.  The equipment slot indicated determines the list displayed in the drop down.  Only items available for that slot will be displayed.  The "Advanced" button will open an additional window where the user can brows
e through a directory tree to find the desired item.  In this case, the items are grouped by type, rather than just appearing in alphabetical order, as in the drop down list.
\par 
\par \tab }{\i\fs20 Name Options ~
\par \tab }{\fs20 Selecting any of the three options, will determine whether the items are displayed in the drop down by template name, screen name, or documentation.  In many cases, the screen name is the same as the documentation name.
\par 
\par }{\i\fs20\ul Inventory:
\par }{\i\fs20 \tab }{\fs20 This tab and the information displayed on it are only of use to objects that are capable of having an inventory.  In those cases, this tab is used to assign inventory items to the selected object.
\par 
\par \tab 
NOTE ~ assigning inventory items is separate from pcontent variables assigned in templates.  Pcontent will generate random items and gold for objects based on the parameters set in their respective templates, but items assigned to inventory here will assi
gn that specific item to the specific object.
\par 
\par \tab }{\i\fs20 Current Inventory ~
\par }{\fs20 \tab This window displays a list of the items currently in the object's inventory.  Each object is displayed by template name, screen name, and documentation.
\par 
\par \tab }{\i\fs20 Available Inventory Items ~
\par \tab }{\fs20 The
 drop down menu allows the user to select each item and then click on the "Add" button to add the item to the object's inventory.  Optionally, the user can use the "Advanced" button and then browse through the directory tree to select items to be added to
 the inventory.
\par 
\par \tab }{\i\fs20 Name Options ~
\par \tab }{\fs20 Selecting any of the three options, will determine whether the items are displayed in the drop down by template name, screen name, or documentation.  In many cases, the screen name is the same as the documentation name.
\par 
\par }{\i\fs20\ul Lighting:
\par }{\i\fs20 \tab }{\fs20 This tab and the information displayed on it are only of use to lights.}{\b\fs20\ul 
\par }\pard \ql \li0\ri0\widctlpar\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0\itap0 {\b\fs20\ul 
\par }\pard \ql \fi-720\li720\ri0\widctlpar\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin720\itap0 {\b\fs20 \tab }{\i\fs20 Direction ~
\par \tab }{\fs20 By entering values for each of the X, Y, and Z-axis, the user can define the direction the light is shining.  Orbit and Azimuth settings are legacy features and can no longer be edited.
\par 
\par \tab }{\i\fs20 Guid ~
\par \tab }{\fs20 This is the globally unique identifier for the light.  It cannot be edited.
\par 
\par \tab }{\i\fs20 Settings ~
\par }\pard \ql \fi-1440\li1440\ri0\widctlpar\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin1440\itap0 {\i\fs20 \tab }{\fs20\ul Inner Radius}{\i\fs20 : }{\fs20 
This measurement indicates the minimum distance at which objects are affected by the light given off by the selected light source.
\par 
\par \tab }{\fs20\ul Outer Radius}{\i\fs20 : }{\fs20 This measurement indicates the maximum distance at which objects are affected by the light given off by the selected light source.
\par 
\par \tab }{\fs20\ul Intensity}{\i\fs20 : }{\fs20 This value is an indicator of the brightness of the light.  It can be any number from 0 to 1.
\par }\pard \ql \li0\ri0\widctlpar\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0\itap0 {\i\fs20\ul 
\par }\pard \ql \fi-720\li720\ri0\widctlpar\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin720\itap0 {\i\fs20 \tab Color Values ~
\par \tab }{\fs20 RGB values can be entered in the fields to designate the color of the selected light.  By clicking on the "Choose Color" button, the user can se
lect the color for the directional light by selecting the color from the palette or by entering a RGB value.  If the "Advanced" button is used, the user can still select the color from a palette or enter RGB values, but also has access to the HSB (Hue, Sa
turation, Brightness) values.
\par 
\par \tab }{\i\fs20 Final Color ~
\par \tab }{\fs20 The box in this field shows the currently indicated color for the selected directional light.
\par 
\par \tab }{\i\fs20 Options ~
\par }\pard \ql \fi-1440\li1440\ri0\widctlpar\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin1440\itap0 {\i\fs20 \tab }{\fs20\ul Occlude:}{\fs20  When this option is enabled, terrain and structures will block the directional light from reac
hing items, actors, terrain, and structures on the opposite side.
\par 
\par \tab }{\fs20\ul Active:}{\fs20  This option can be toggled on or off to activate or disable the selected directional light.
\par 
\par \tab }{\fs20\ul Affects Items:}{\fs20  When enabled, directional lights will affect the appearance of items.
\par 
\par \tab }{\fs20\ul Draw Shadows:}{\fs20  When enabled, actors affected by directional lights will cast shadows.
\par 
\par \tab }{\fs20\ul Affects Actors:}{\fs20  When enabled, directional lights will affect the appearance of actors (characters, monsters, NPCs).
\par 
\par \tab }{\fs20\ul Affects Terrain:}{\fs20  When enabled, directional lights will affect the appearance of terrain and structures.
\par 
\par \tab }{\fs20\ul On Timer:}{\fs20  When enabled, the directional lights will change intensity and hue to simulate the passage of daytime to nighttime and back.  The directional light settings defined will correspond to the ti
me of day indicated in the "Map Settings" (in the Settings Menu) and will change intensity and hue accordingly as the hour of the day progresses.
\par }\pard \ql \fi-720\li720\ri0\widctlpar\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin720\itap0 {\fs20 
