\par }{\f1\fs20 \page }{\cs19\f1\fs20\super #{\footnote \pard\plain \s18\ql \li0\ri0\widctlpar\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0\itap0 \fs20\lang1033\langfe1033\cgrid\langnp1033\langfenp1033 {\cs19\super #}{ HIDD_DIALOG_MOVE_LOCK}}}{\b\fs20\ul 
Movement Locking:
\par 
\par }\pard \ql \li0\ri0\nowidctlpar\faauto\rin0\lin0\itap0 {\fs20 This tool can disables object movement along any one of the X, Y, or Z axis.  This is useful for fine tuning of object placement.
\par }\pard \ql \li0\ri0\widctlpar\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0\itap0 {\fs20 \page }{\cs19\fs20\super #{\footnote \pard\plain \s18\ql \li0\ri0\widctlpar\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0\itap0 
\fs20\lang1033\langfe1033\cgrid\langnp1033\langfenp1033 {\cs19\super #}{ HIDD_DIALOG_COMMAND_EDITOR}}}{\b\fs20\ul Command Editor:
\par 
\par }{\fs20 This tool is used to issue simple movement and patrol commands.  Once issued, if needed, the commands can be further edited in their respective properties dialogue windows.  The commands are placed by selecting the actor, who will receive the co
mmand(s), then selecting the type of command to place, and then placing the command(s).
\par 
\par NOTE ~ by selecting the actor, then placing the commands, the commands will automatically be linked according to the order of their placement.  However, if edits are n
eeded to add or remove commands, or if commands are placed without first selecting an actor, it is possible to link them together by using the Link button (see below).
\par 
\par }{\i\fs20\ul Tools:
\par }{\i\fs20 \tab Select ~
\par \tab }{\fs20 When this button is in the down position, the cursor can be used to select items without accidentally placing commands.
\par 
\par \tab }{\i\fs20 Link ~
\par }\pard \ql \fi-720\li720\ri0\widctlpar\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin720\itap0 {\i\fs20 \tab }{\fs20 
Once a command is placed without a link to an actor or prior command, the link button can be used to link the desired objects together by selecting the object being linked FROM, then clicking the Link button, then selecting the object to be linked TO.

\par 
\par }{\i\fs20\ul Command:
\par }{\fs20 \tab }{\i\fs20 Move ~
\par \tab }{\fs20 When this button is in the down position, the user can place movement commands by simply clicking the left mouse button.  In each case, the command placed is represented by a blue 
star and indicates the point to which the selected actor will move.   The placing of subsequent move commands will be linked to the previous command and will be the next point the actor travels to after reaching the prior command point.  There is no limit
 to the number of command points that can be assigned to an actor, but the movement can be disturbed by other influences (attacking, defending, etc.).
\par 
\par \tab }{\i\fs20 Patrol ~
\par \tab }{\fs20 
The patrol command is placed and functions in the same manner as the move command.  One major difference is that when patrol points are placed, the most recent command placed automatically links to the first patrol command that was placed.  This forms a p
atrol pattern that will be looped continuously until the actor is broken out of the patrol.  This is unlike the movement commands, where the actor goes through the movement commands and stops at the last one.
\par }\pard \ql \li0\ri0\widctlpar\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0\itap0 {\fs20 
\par }{\i\fs20\ul Related Editor Dialogue Windows:
\par }{\fs20 View ~> Preferences
\par Object ~> Object Properties
\par 
\par }{\i\fs20\ul Related Siege University Courses:
\par }{\fs20 102: Objects
\par 200: Dungeon Siege Concepts and Terminology
\par 207: Non-Interactive Sequences (NIS)
\par 304: Scripted Sequences
\par }{\f1\fs20 \page }{\cs19\f1\fs20\super #{\footnote \pard\plain \s18\ql \li0\ri0\widctlpar\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0\itap0 \fs20\lang1033\langfe1033\cgrid\langnp1033\langfenp1033 {\cs19\super #}{ HIDD_DIALOG_PARTY_EDITOR}}}{\b\fs20\ul 
Party Editor:
\par 
\par }{\fs20 The party editor is used 
to group NPCs together.  Its main purpose at this time is to set up an NPC with the ability to sell packmules. In order for the user to use the Party Editor's functionality, there muse first be a Party Gizmo available for editing.  Siege Editor versions p
rior to 1.09.2 will need to use the content search for "party_humanoid", as the Party Gizmo does not appear in the object list until v. 1.09.2.
\par 
\par }{\i\fs20\ul Party Name:
\par }{\fs20 Once a Party Gizmo has been placed, this drop down list can be used to select the name of the gizmo to indicate which party is being formed.  In this case, the name of the gizmo will be its SCID.
\par 
\par }{\i\fs20\ul Members:
\par }{\fs20 This window lists the Fuel Name and SCID for each actor currently assigned to the Party Gizmo.  By selecting an actor and hitting the "Remove" button,
 the selected actor can be removed from the party.  Also, by hitting the "Remove All" button, all actors can be removed from the party.
\par 
\par }{\i\fs20\ul Available Actors:
\par }{\fs20 This window lists all of the actors in the region by Fuel Name and SCID.  By selecting a specific actor and hitting the "<-Add" button, the selected actor will be added to the Party Gizmo indicated by the Party Name drop down list.
\par 
\par }{\i\fs20\ul Related Siege University Courses:
\par }{\fs20 101: Nodework
\par 102: Objects
\par 106: Monster Placement
\par 208: Non-Player Characters
\par 306: Stores
\par }{\f1\fs20 \page }{\cs19\f1\fs20\super #{\footnote \pard\plain \s18\ql \li0\ri0\widctlpar\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0\itap0 \fs20\lang1033\langfe1033\cgrid\langnp1033\langfenp1033 {\cs19\super #}{ HIDD_DIALOG_OBJECT_GROUPING}}}{
\b\fs20\ul Object Grouping:
\par 
\par }{\fs20 This tool is used to aid in the manipulation of groups of objects by using broad pre-specified groups, or by using user-defined groups.
\par 
\par }{\i\fs20\ul Groups:
\par }{\fs20 This window displays a directory tree structure of the various
 groups.  Metagroups are broad range groups, Categories are more specified, and Groups is empty to start with, but will contain any user-defined groups.  When a particular group is selected and the "Select Group" button is clicked, all members of that gro
up will be highlighted and can then be manipulated by the various buttons.
\par 
\par NOTE ~ In order to select a group, the group must be selected with the checkbox AND the root must also be selected.  For example, to select the "Actors" group, "Actors" must be checked AND "Metagroup" must be checked.
\par 
\par }{\i\fs20\ul Selection Commands:
\par }{\i\fs20 \tab Rotate Group Mode ~
\par }\pard \ql \fi-720\li720\ri0\widctlpar\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin720\itap0 {\i\fs20 \tab }{\fs20 Each time this button is clicked, all highlighted object will be rotated }{\i\fs20 as a single group }{\fs20 around a single axis point.

\par 
\par \tab }{\i\fs20 Random Rotate ~
\par \tab }{\fs20 Each time this button is clicked, each highlighted object will rotate to a random facing around its own central axis.
\par 
\par \tab }{\i\fs20 Show Selection ~
\par \tab }{\fs20 Clicking on this button will reveal any of the checked groups that were previously hidden.
\par 
\par \tab }{\i\fs20 Hide Selection ~
\par \tab }{\fs20 Clicking on this button will hide any checked groups from view.  This is very useful when trying to place objects into cluttered areas.
\par 
\par \tab }{\i\fs20 Scale ~
\par \tab }{\fs20 Entering a minimum and maximum size possibility and then clicking on the \'93Random Scale\'94
 button will randomly resize each selected object resized within the minimum and maximum size settings.  The default size of an object is a size of 1.
\par 
\par }\pard \ql \li0\ri0\widctlpar\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0\itap0 {\i\fs20\ul Select All:
\par }{\fs20 When one of the root group sets is highlighted, this button will automatically select (check) all of the sub group sets under that heading.}{\i\fs20\ul 
\par }\pard \ql \fi-720\li720\ri0\widctlpar\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin720\itap0 {\i\fs20\ul 
\par Deselect All:
\par }\pard \ql \li0\ri0\widctlpar\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0\itap0 {\fs20 When one of the root group sets is highlighted, this button will automatically deselect (uncheck) all of the sub group sets under that heading.}{\i\fs20\ul 
\par }\pard \ql \fi-720\li720\ri0\widctlpar\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin720\itap0 {\i\fs20\ul 
\par }\pard \ql \li0\ri0\widctlpar\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0\itap0 {\i\fs20\ul New:
\par }{\fs20 The button will create a new group under the Groups heading in list.  It will also open another dialogue window prompting the user to name the new group.}{\i\fs20\ul 
\par 
\par Add:
\par }{\fs20 When a group set is highlighted, new objects can be added to the group by selecting the object in the Main Window of the editor and then cl
icking on the "Add" button.  The user will then be prompted to verify that is the addition is intentional.  It is possible for an object to be in more than one group.
\par }{\i\fs20\ul 
\par Remove:
\par }{\fs20 When a group set is highlighted, objects can be removed from the group by selecting the object in the Main Window of the editor and then clicking on the "Remove" button.  The user will then be prompted to verify that is the removal is intentional.

\par 
\par }{\i\fs20\ul Show:
\par }{\fs20 Unlike the \'93Show Selected\'94 button, which will make any group that is checked in the directory tree visible, this button will only affect a group that is highlighted in the Main Window.
\par }{\i\fs20\ul 
\par Hide:
\par }{\fs20 Unlike the \'93Hide Selected\'94 button, which will hide any group that is checked in the directory tree, this button will only affect a group that is highlighted in the Main Window.
\par }{\i\fs20\ul 
\par Select Group:
\par }{\fs20 Once a group is selected in the "Groups" list, all objects in this group will be highlighted in the Main Window of the editor once this button is clicked.  Objects must be selected and highlighted in the Main 
Window to be manipulated by any of the other buttons in the Object Grouping dialogue window.}{\i\fs20\ul 
\par 
\par Deselect Group:
\par }{\fs20 This button will deselect previously selected groups that are indicated in the Groups list.
\par 
\par }{\i\fs20\ul Related Siege University Courses:
\par }{\fs20 102: Objects
\par 106: Monster Placement
\par 200: Dungeon Siege Concepts and Terminology
\par }{\f1\fs20 \page }{\cs19\f1\fs20\super #{\footnote \pard\plain \s18\ql \li0\ri0\widctlpar\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0\itap0 \fs20\lang1033\langfe1033\cgrid\langnp1033\langfenp1033 {\cs19\super #}{ HIDD_DIALOG_MACROS}}}{\b\fs20\ul Macros:

\par 
\par }{\fs20 This tool is used to save time and effort when a group of objects will be used multiple times.  For example, if the user created a series of fade nodes, spen
ding a lot of time entering data, commands, properties, etc., and planned to use the same triggers in another area, a macro of the trigger group could be created so that the user could place the whole group multiple times, as needed.
\par 
\par }{\i\fs20\ul Macros:
\par }{\fs20 This is a list of the macro names currently in existance.
\par 
\par }{\i\fs20\ul Define Macro:
\par }{\fs20 To determine what objects make up the macro, select the items in the editor's Main Window.  (cntrl+click and click and drag methods can be used to select multiple objects.)  Then click on the "Def
ine Macro" button.  This will bring up a dialogue window prompting the user to enter a name for the macro.  Once entered, the macro name will appear in the Macros window.
\par 
\par }{\i\fs20\ul Delete Macro:
\par }{\fs20 Selecting a macro in the Macros window and then clicking on the "Delete Macro" button will remove the macro from the Macros window.
\par 
\par }{\i\fs20\ul Place Macro In Node:
\par }{\fs20 To place a copy of an existing macro in the world, select the macro from the Macros list and then click the "Place Macro In Node" button.  The macro can then be placed by m
oving the cursor to the Main Window of the editor and clicking on the map where the macro is to be added.
\par 
\par }\pard\plain \s1\ql \li0\ri0\keepn\nowidctlpar\faauto\outlinelevel0\rin0\lin0\itap0 \f1\fs24\lang1033\langfe1033\cgrid\langnp1033\langfenp1033 {\i\fs20\ul Related Siege University Courses:
\par }\pard\plain \ql \li0\ri0\widctlpar\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0\itap0 \fs24\lang1033\langfe1033\cgrid\langnp1033\langfenp1033 {\fs20 101: Nodework
\par 102: Objects
\par 106: Monster Placement
\par 200: Dungeon Siege Concepts and Terminology
\par }{\f1\fs20 \page }{\cs19\f1\fs20\super #{\footnote \pard\plain \s18\ql \li0\ri0\widctlpar\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0\itap0 \fs20\lang1033\langfe1033\cgrid\langnp1033\langfenp1033 {\cs19\super #}{ HIDD_DIALOG_OBJECT_LIST}}}{\b\fs20\ul 
Object List:
\par 
\par }{\fs20 This dialogue window displays a list of
 all objects in the region.  Individual items can be highlighted and then if the "Select" button can be used, the Siege Editor window will center its view on that item.  The "Delete" button can also be used in conjunction with highlighted items to delete 
objects from the map.
\par }{\f1\fs20 \page }{\cs19\f1\fs20\super #{\footnote \pard\plain \s18\ql \li0\ri0\widctlpar\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0\itap0 \fs20\lang1033\langfe1033\cgrid\langnp1033\langfenp1033 {\cs19\super #}{ HIDD_DIALOG_CONTENT_SEARCH}}}{\b\fs20\ul 
Template Content Search:
\par 
\par }{\fs20 This tool searches the templates to aid the user in finding the template name for specific actors and items.  After the desired template is found, it can be double clicked on the list to select it, and then it can be placed in the editor\rquote 
s Main Window.
\par 
\par }{\i\fs20\ul Search Parameters:
\par }{\i\fs20 \tab Template Contains Text ~
\par }\pard \ql \fi-720\li720\ri0\widctlpar\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin720\itap0 {\i\fs20 \tab }{\fs20 Any entry in this field will return a list of all instances of that entry in any template.  For example, entering "krug" into the fiel
d, will list each of the types of krug by their template names.  NOTE ~ in many cases, the template name is the same as the description, so entering information in this field will generate many of the same results as the same information entered in the "D
escription Contains Text".
\par 
\par \tab }{\i\fs20 Screen Name Contains Text ~
\par \tab }{\fs20 Any entry in this field will return a list of all instances of templates sharing the indicated screen name.  NOTE ~ Not all items have screen names.
\par 
\par \tab }{\i\fs20 Description Contains Text ~
\par \tab }{\fs20 Any entry in this f
ield will return a list of all instances of templates that contain the entered data in their description fields.  For example, entering "krug" into the field, will list each of the types of krug.  NOTE ~ in many cases, the description is the same as the t
emplate name, so entering information in this field will generate many of the same results as the same information entered in the "Template Contains Text".
\par 
\par \tab }{\i\fs20 Specializes ~
\par \tab }{\fs20 When a template is designated to inherit the specifications of a broader category "b
ase" template, it is said to "specialize" that base template.  For example, the template "krug_grunt_boss_01" inherits many of its properties form the "base_krug" template.  Thus, the krug_grunt_boss_01 template "specializes" the base_krug template.  Usin
g the drop down menu, the user can select from a list of templates and see a list of all templates that specialize the chosen template.
\par 
\par }{\i\fs20\ul Add to Custom:
\par }\pard \ql \li0\ri0\widctlpar\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0\itap0 {\fs20 Clicking on the "Add to Custom" button will add all templates listed in the Search Results window to the custom file tab of the File Window in the Siege Editor.
\par 
\par }{\i\fs20\ul Search Results:
\par }{\fs20 This window displays the list of templates that meet the search criteria.  The window shows the template name, the screen name (if one exists), and the description indicator.
\par 
\par }{\i\fs20\ul Related Editor Dialogue Windows:
\par }{\fs20 Object ~> Object Properties
\par Object ~> Object Grouping
\par Object ~> Object Macros
\par Object ~> Object List
\par 
\par }{\i\fs20\ul Related Siege University Courses:
\par }{\fs20 102: Objects
\par 200: Dungeon Siege Concepts and Terminology
\par 201: Templates
\par 302: Templates II
\par }{\f1\fs20 \page }{\cs19\f1\fs20\super #{\footnote \pard\plain \s18\ql \li0\ri0\widctlpar\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0\itap0 \fs20\lang1033\langfe1033\cgrid\langnp1033\langfenp1033 {\cs19\super #}{ HIDD_DIALOG_LIGHTING_AMBIENCE}}}{
\b\fs20\ul Ambient Lighting
\par 
\par }{\fs20 This dialogue window is used to edit ambient light settings. Ambient light is not light in the conventional sense.  It has no direction or light source, and casts no shadow.  Instead, ambient light takes eve
ry surface and sets each surface to the brightness and color as described in the ambient light settings.
\par 
\par NOTE ~ to see the effect of changes in ambient light, be sure that "Ignore Vertex Colors" is disabled in the preferences settings(under the View Menu).}{\b\fs20\ul 
\par 
\par }{\i\fs20\ul Abient Intensity:
\par }{\fs20 By entering intensity values for ambient lighting, users can control the brightness of ambient lights on actors, objects, and miscellaneous general items.  Intensity can be set from 0.0 (no intensity) to 1.0 (full intensity). 
\par 
\par }{\i\fs20\ul Ambient Color:
\par }{\fs20 
Just as the intensity of ambient light can be set for each of the actor, object, and general categories, the ambient color can also be set.  Selecting a color can be done in one of two ways.  The color can be entered into the data field in its hexi
decimal representation, or, by clicking on the corresponding "Color..." button, the user can select the color directly from a color palette or enter its RBG, hue, and saturation values manually.
\par 
\par }{\i\fs20\ul Update:
\par }{\fs20 After editing ambient light settings, the "Update" button must be clicked for the changes to take effect.
\par 
\par }{\i\fs20\ul Related Editor Dialogue Windows:
\par }{\fs20 Objects ~> Object Properties
\par 
\par }{\i\fs20\ul Related Siege University Courses:
\par }{\fs20 103: Lighting
\par 200: Dungeon Siege Concepts and Terminology
\par 203A: Triggers
\par 203B: Triggers II
\par 204: Moods
\par 207: Non-Interactive Sequences (NIS)
\par }{\f1\fs20 \page }{\cs19\f1\fs20\super #{\footnote \pard\plain \s18\ql \li0\ri0\widctlpar\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0\itap0 \fs20\lang1033\langfe1033\cgrid\langnp1033\langfenp1033 {\cs19\super #}{ HIDD_DIALOG_LIGHTING_EDIT_DIRECTIONAL}}}{
\b\fs20\ul Edit Directional Lights
\par 
\par }{\fs20 With this dialogue window, users can create, edit, and remove di
rectional lights.  Directional lights, while not originating from a specific source or point of origin, do have a direction, color and intensity associated with them.  They cover the entire region and affect all surfaces they "hit".  Sunlight for a region
 would be a good example of directional light.
\par 
\par Enabling "Draw Directional Lights" under the Lighting Menu will draw directional light indicators on the map in Siege Editor.  The indicator will look like a series of small hashmarks across the region.  The c
olor and direction of the directional light will be mirrored in the color and direction of the hashmark.
\par 
\par }{\i\fs20\ul New:
\par }{\fs20 This button will add a new directional light to the region.  A corresponding hexadecimal number will show up in the list to the left of the "New" button to identify the new light.
\par 
\par }{\i\fs20\ul Edit:
\par }{\fs20 By selecting a directional light by highlighting its corresponding hexadecimal identifier from the light list and then clicking on "Edit" will take you to the Directional Light Properties dialogue window where the 
user can edit the settings and characteristics of the selected directional light.
\par 
\par }{\i\fs20\ul Remove:
\par }{\fs20 Selecting a light from the light list and clicking on the "Remove" button will delete the light from the list and from the region.
\par 
\par }{\i\fs20\ul Update All:
\par }{\fs20 The "Update All" button will activate any changes made to the settings of any of the directional lights in the list.
\par 
\par }{\i\fs20\ul Related Editor Dialogue Windows:
\par }{\fs20 Objects ~> Object Properties
\par Lighting ~> Edit Direction Lights ~> Directional Light Properties
\par 
\par }{\i\fs20\ul Related Siege University Courses:
\par }{\fs20 103: Lighting
\par }{\f1\fs20 \page }{\cs19\f1\fs20\super #{\footnote \pard\plain \s18\ql \li0\ri0\widctlpar\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0\itap0 \fs20\lang1033\langfe1033\cgrid\langnp1033\langfenp1033 {\cs19\super #}{ directional_light_properties}}}{
\b\fs20\ul Directional Light Properties:
\par 
\par }{\i\fs20\ul Color:
\par }\pard \ql \fi-720\li720\ri0\widctlpar\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin720\itap0 {\i\fs20 \tab Color Value ~
\par \tab }{\fs20 Using Vector Values, a color for the selected directional light can be entered manually.  Vector values function similarly to RGB values; but the values are between 0.0 and 1.0.
\par 
\par \tab }{\i\fs20 Color Selection ~
\par \tab }{\fs20 By selecting the "Standard" button, the user can select the color for the directional light by selecting the color from the palette or by entering a RGB value.  If the "Advanced" button is used, the user can
 still select the color from a palette or enter RGB values, but also has access to the HSB (Hue, Saturation, Brightness) values.
\par 
\par \tab }{\i\fs20 Final Color ~
\par \tab }{\fs20 The box in this field shows the currently indicated color for the selected directional light.
\par 
\par }\pard \ql \li0\ri0\widctlpar\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0\itap0 {\i\fs20\ul Lighting:
\par }\pard \ql \fi-720\li720\ri0\widctlpar\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin720\itap0 {\fs20 \tab }{\i\fs20 Direction ~
\par \tab }{\fs20 By entering values for each of the X, Y, and Z-axis, the user can define the direction the light is shining.  Orbit and Azimuth settings are legacy features and can no longer be edited.
\par 
\par \tab }{\i\fs20 Intensity ~
\par \tab }{\fs20 This is a numerical representation of the brightness of the directional light.  In this case, 0 is the minimum setting, and 1.0 is full intensity.
\par 
\par \tab }{\i\fs20 Options ~
\par }\pard \ql \fi-1440\li1440\ri0\widctlpar\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin1440\itap0 {\i\fs20 \tab }{\fs20\ul Occlude:}{\fs20 
 When this option is enabled, terrain and structures will block the directional light from reaching items, actors, terrain, and structures on the opposite side.
\par 
\par \tab }{\fs20\ul Acitve:}{\fs20  This option can be toggled on or off to activate or disable the selected directional light.
\par 
\par \tab }{\fs20\ul Affects Items:}{\fs20  When enabled, directional lights will affect the appearance of items.
\par 
\par \tab }{\fs20\ul Draw Shadows:}{\fs20  When enabled, actors affected by directional lights will cast shadows.
\par 
\par \tab }{\fs20\ul Affects Actors:}{\fs20  When enabled, directional lights will affect the appearance of actors (characters, monsters, NPCs).
\par 
\par \tab }{\fs20\ul Affects Terrain:}{\fs20  When enabled, directional lights will affect the appearance of terrain and structures.
\par 
\par \tab }{\fs20\ul On Timer:}{\fs20  When enabled, the directional lights will change intensity and direction to simulate the passage of daytime to nighttime and back.  The directional light settings defined will correspond to the time of day ind
icated in the "Map Settings" (in the Settings Menu) and will change intensity and direction accordingly as the hour of the day progresses.
\par 
\par }\pard\plain \s1\ql \fi-1440\li1440\ri0\keepn\nowidctlpar\faauto\outlinelevel0\rin0\lin1440\itap0 \f1\fs24\lang1033\langfe1033\cgrid\langnp1033\langfenp1033 {\i\fs20\ul Related Editor Dialogue Windows:
\par }\pard\plain \ql \li0\ri0\widctlpar\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0\itap0 \fs24\lang1033\langfe1033\cgrid\langnp1033\langfenp1033 {\fs20 Objects ~> Object Properties
\par Lighting ~> Edit Directional Lights
\par 
\par }{\i\fs20\ul Related Siege University Courses:
\par }{\fs20 103: Lighting
\par 200: Dungeon Siege Concepts and Terminology
\par }{\f1\fs20 \page }{\cs19\f1\fs20\super #{\footnote \pard\plain \s18\ql \li0\ri0\widctlpar\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0\itap0 \fs20\lang1033\langfe1033\cgrid\langnp1033\langfenp1033 {\cs19\super #}{ HIDD_DIALOG_MAP_SETTINGS}}}{\b\fs20\ul 
Map Settings:
\par 
\par }{\i\fs20\ul Name:
\par }{\fs20 The name of the map as it appears in the directory.
\par 
\par }{\i\fs20\ul Screen Name:
\par }{\fs20 The name of the map as it appears on the in-game map list.
\par 
\par }{\i\fs20\ul Description:
\par }{\fs20 The information displayed in the description text box when the map is selected from the in-game list.
\par 
\par }{\i\fs20\ul Camera Settings:
\par }\pard \ql \fi-720\li720\ri0\widctlpar\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin720\itap0 {\fs20 \tab This area serves two purposes.  It sets up the starting position for the camera when a new game is started, and it sets pa
rameters for camera mobility in-game. NOTE ~ if the set starting position of the camera does not fall within the parameters set by the other settings, the editor will automatically adjust the starting position to a \'93legal\'94 position.
\par }\pard \ql \li0\ri0\widctlpar\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0\itap0 {\fs20 
\par }\pard \ql \li720\ri0\widctlpar\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin720\itap0 {\i\fs20 North, East, Up ~
\par }{\fs20 These settings correspond to the X, Y, and Z-axis and signify the starting position of the camera in relation to the selected node.
\par }{\i\fs20 
\par }\pard\plain \s1\ql \li720\ri0\keepn\nowidctlpar\faauto\outlinelevel0\rin0\lin720\itap0 \f1\fs24\lang1033\langfe1033\cgrid\langnp1033\langfenp1033 {\i\fs20 Distance, Azimuth, Orbit, FOV  ~
\par }{\fs20 In each case, the values in these fields can be used to adjust the distance, angle, orientation and Field of Vision of the camera for its starting position in relation to the selected node.}{\i\fs20  
\par }\pard\plain \ql \li0\ri0\widctlpar\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0\itap0 \fs24\lang1033\langfe1033\cgrid\langnp1033\langfenp1033 {
\par \tab }{\i\fs20 Use Current Camera Position ~
\par }\pard \ql \fi-720\li720\ri0\widctlpar\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin720\itap0 {\i\fs20 \tab }{\fs20 By using this button, the user, 
instead of manually entering all the data above, can instead simply place the camera in the desired position and click on the button to set the camera\rquote s starting position.
\par }\pard \ql \li0\ri0\widctlpar\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0\itap0 {\i\fs20 
\par }\pard \ql \li720\ri0\widctlpar\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin720\itap0 {\i\fs20 Node ~
\par }{\fs20 This field displays the current node being used as the reference point by th
e starting camera position settings. The starting camera position settings are set in relation to the selected node.  By changing the node, the settings will persist, but the actual location of the camera will change to reflect the settings in relation to
 the node. The user can change the selected node by entering a new node ID here.
\par 
\par }\pard \ql \fi720\li0\ri0\widctlpar\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0\itap0 {\i\fs20 Use Selected Node ~
\par }\pard \ql \li720\ri0\widctlpar\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin720\itap0 {\fs20 Instead of manually entering the referenced node ID, the user can, instead, select the desired node on in the editor\rquote s Main Window, and then click on this 
button to set the referenced node.
\par 
\par }{\i\fs20 Minimum Distance ~
\par }{\fs20 This is a global setting for the minimum distance the camera can scroll in relation to the map.
\par }\pard \ql \li0\ri0\widctlpar\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0\itap0 {\i\fs20 
\par }\pard \ql \li720\ri0\widctlpar\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin720\itap0 {\i\fs20 Maximum Distance ~
\par }{\fs20 This is a global setting for the maximum distance the camera can scroll out in relation to the map.
\par 
\par }{\i\fs20 Minimum Azimuth ~
\par }{\fs20 This is a global setting indicating, in degrees, the shallowest angle to which the camera can tilt down.
\par }{\i\fs20 
\par Maximum Azimuth ~
\par }{\fs20 This is the global setting indicating, in degrees, the steepest angle to which the camera can tilt up.
\par 
\par }{\i\fs20 Near Clip ~
\par }{\fs20 This is a global setting indicating, in meters, how close an object can be before it \'93clips\'94 or leaves the field of vision.  (i.e. - the character passes through it.)
\par }{\i\fs20 
\par Far Clip ~
\par }\pard \ql \fi-720\li720\ri0\widctlpar\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin720\itap0 {\i\fs20 \tab }{\fs20 This is a global setting indicating, in meters, how far away an object can be before it disappears from the field of vision.
\par }\pard \ql \li720\ri0\widctlpar\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin720\itap0 {\i\fs20 
\par }\pard \ql \li0\ri0\widctlpar\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0\itap0 {\i\fs20\ul Advanced:
\par }{\i\fs20 \tab Starting time of day ~
\par }\pard \ql \li720\ri0\widctlpar\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin720\itap0 {\fs20 Using a 24-Hour clock, the user can set the time of day in effect whe
n a new game is started.  This affects timed lights as they brighten and darken according to the time of day to simulate the passage of time.
\par 
\par }{\i\fs20 World frustum radius ~}{\i\fs20\ul 
\par }{\fs20 Dungeon Siege uses a process by which information and parts of the world are constantly st
reamed in and out.  The frustum is a circle around a given character that represents the part of the map that the computer currently has loaded in memory.  By adjusting the radius of this circle, the user can control the amount of information available to
 a character at any given time.  If no number is entered here, the radius measurement will default to 45 meters.
\par 
\par }{\i\fs20 World Interest Radius ~}{\i\fs20\ul 
\par }{\fs20 Each object can be assigned an interest radius that is measured in meters.  This number indicates how close a character
 must be to the node that an object is on for the object to be visible to the player.  Objects with no specified interest radius will default to the world interest radius.
\par 
\par }{\i\fs20 Development-Only Map ~
\par }\pard \ql \fi720\li0\ri0\widctlpar\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0\itap0 {\fs20 If checked, the map will not show up on the in-game map list.
\par 
\par }\pard \ql \li0\ri0\widctlpar\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0\itap0 {\i\fs20\ul Related Siege University Courses:
\par }{\fs20 103: Lighting
\par 200: Dungeon Siege Concepts and Terminology
\par }{\f1\fs20 \page }{\cs19\f1\fs20\super #{\footnote \pard\plain \s18\ql \li0\ri0\widctlpar\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0\itap0 \fs20\lang1033\langfe1033\cgrid\langnp1033\langfenp1033 {\cs19\super #}{ HIDD_DIALOG_REGION_SETTINGS}}}{
\b\fs20\ul Region Settings:
\par 
\par }{\i\fs20\ul Name:
\par }{\fs20 The name of the region as it appears in the directory.
\par 
\par }{\i\fs20\ul Description:
\par }{\fs20 The name of the region as it appears in-game.
\par 
\par }{\i\fs20\ul Environment Map:
\par }{\fs20 Though rarely used (in Dungeon Siege it was only used for Gom), this drop down menu is used to select which bitmap is used on surfaces to create a sense of the surface being \'93reflective\'94.  Bit
maps available for use in this way are located in the \\art\\Bitmaps\\Envmaps folder.
\par }{\i\fs20\ul 
\par Region GUID:
\par }{\fs20 The GUID (Globally Unique Identifier) assigned to the region.
\par 
\par }{\i\fs20\ul Related Siege University Courses:
\par }{\fs20 200: Dungeon Siege Concepts and Terminology
\par }{\f1\fs20 \page }{\cs19\f1\fs20\super #{\footnote \pard\plain \s18\ql \li0\ri0\widctlpar\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0\itap0 \fs20\lang1033\langfe1033\cgrid\langnp1033\langfenp1033 {\cs19\super #}{ HIDD_DIALOG_BOOKMARK_LIST}}}{\b\fs20\ul 
Bookmark Editor:
\par 
\par }{\fs20 Bookmarks are developmental tools whereby a teleport destination point is set for in-game developmental use.
\par 
\par NOTE ~ the teleport command is only usable with the RELEASE version of the DungeonSiege.exe.  It cannot be used with the RETAIL version of the .exe.
\par 
\par }{\i\fs20\ul Available Bookmarks:
\par }{\fs20 This is a list of all of the bookmarks in the entire map.  They are named by the user and it is suggested that they be named in such a way that their location is easy to remember - by region name, for instance.
\par 
\par }{\i\fs20\ul New:
\par }{\fs20 This button creates a new bookmark entry in the Available Bookmarks list and opens up the Bookmark Properties dialogue window.
\par 
\par }{\i\fs20\ul Properties:
\par }{\fs20 Selecting a bookmark from the list and hitting this button will open the Bookmark Properties dialogue window and display the property information for the selected bookmark.
\par 
\par }{\i\fs20\ul Remove:
\par }{\fs20 Selecting a bookmark from the list and clicking on this button will delete the bookmark and its entry on the Available Bookmarks list.
\par 
\par }{\i\fs20\ul Related Editor Dialogue Windows:
\par }{\fs20 Settings ~> Bookmark Editor ~> Bookmark Properties
\par }{\f1\fs20 \page }{\cs19\f1\fs20\super #{\footnote \pard\plain \s18\ql \li0\ri0\widctlpar\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0\itap0 \fs20\lang1033\langfe1033\cgrid\langnp1033\langfenp1033 {\cs19\super #}{ HIDD_DIALOG_BOOKMARK_PROPERTIES}}}{
\b\fs20\ul Bookmark Properties:
\par 
\par }{\i\fs20\ul Name:
\par }{\fs20 This is the name of the bookmark.  It is suggested that the bookmark be named in such a way as to make it easy to remember where it leads - using the region name, for example.
\par 
\par }{\i\fs20\ul Position:
\par }{\fs20 Users can indicate the position of the bookmark by manually entering the node GUID, and the X,Y,Z axis information, or by highlighting a node in the editor's Main Window and then clicking the "Use Selected Node" button.
\par 
\par }{\i\fs20\ul Camera Settings:
\par }{\fs20 Users can indicate the position 
of the camera upon reaching the bookmark destination via teleporting by either manually entering the Distance, Azimuth, and Orbit values, or by setting the camera into the desired position in the editor's Main Window and then clicking the "Use Current Cam
era Position" button.
\par 
\par }{\i\fs20\ul Description:
\par }{\fs20 Any descriptive information entered here will show up on the screen along with the bookmark name when console commands are used in the release version of the DungeonSiege.exe to generate a list of the bookmarks.
\par 
\par }{\i\fs20\ul Related Editor Dialogue Windows:
\par }{\fs20 Settings ~> Bookmark Editor
\par }{\f1\fs20 \page }{\cs19\f1\fs20\super #{\footnote \pard\plain \s18\ql \li0\ri0\widctlpar\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0\itap0 \fs20\lang1033\langfe1033\cgrid\langnp1033\langfenp1033 {\cs19\super #}{ HIDD_DIALOG_REGION_HOTPOINT_DIRECTION}}}{
\b\fs20\ul Edit North Vector:
\par 
\par }{\fs20 Setting the North Vector is required for the functionality of the in-game compass.  The North Vector must be set for each region in a
 map and can point in any of 360 degrees.  It is possible to enter the North Vector information in two ways.  The user can enter X and Z-axis values, or use the drop down menu to select the orbit heading.  The X and Z-axis values can each be any value fro
m
 -1 to 1, and the orbit is any number from 0 to 359, indicating the degree heading.  Once entered, the North Vector will be displayed as a purple line leading from the center of the map toward the direction indicated as North.  The North Vector display wi
ll only be visible when the Edit North Vector window is open.
\par 
\par }{\i\fs20\ul Related Editor Dialogue Windows:
\par }{\fs20 Lighting ~> Ambient Lighting
\par 
\par }\pard\plain \s1\ql \li0\ri0\keepn\nowidctlpar\faauto\outlinelevel0\rin0\lin0\itap0 \f1\fs24\lang1033\langfe1033\cgrid\langnp1033\langfenp1033 {\i\fs20\ul Related Siege University Courses:
