\fs20\lang1033\langfe1033\cgrid\langnp1033\langfenp1033 {\cs19\super #}{ HIDD_DIALOG_SFX}}}{\b\fs20\ul Siege Fx Editor:
\par 
\par }{\fs20 This tool is used to create and define the scripts used to create simple special effects such as flames, smoke, and spell effects. 
\par 
\par NOTE ~ while this help file describes the use of the various functions of this tool, prior familiarity with the use of scripts and the components that make up the scripts will aid in the understanding of the tool's functionality.
\par 
\par To begin editing a script, the user can use the "New Script" button to create a new script or the "Load Script" button, which open a drop down list from which the user can select the script to be edited.
\par 
\par }{\i\fs20\ul Script ~ :
\par }{\fs20 This window shows the name of the script
 being edited and all of the commands and parameters, which make up the script.  As commands and parameters are added, values that can be selected from a drop down list will be highlighted in light blue, while values that must be entered directly will hav
e a white background.
\par 
\par }{\i\fs20\ul Add Command:
\par }{\fs20 This button will add another command line to the "Script ~" window.  The user can then use the drop down list in the "Command" cell to define what type of command is being entered.  The type of command will automatically 
define what parameter cells are displayed and available to be edited.
\par 
\par }{\i\fs20\ul Edit Parameters:
\par }{\fs20 This button will open another dialogue window where the selected command's parameters can be edited.
\par 
\par }{\i\fs20\ul Remove Command:
\par }{\fs20 When this button is clicked, the selected command will be removed from the "Script ~" window.
\par 
\par }{\i\fs20\ul Testing:
\par }{\fs20 By selecting objects to be the source and destination of the special effect, the user can test the graphic effect in the editor.
\par 
\par \tab }{\i\fs20 Source ~
\par }\pard \ql \fi-720\li720\ri0\widctlpar\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin720\itap0 {\i\fs20 \tab }{\fs20 This is the origin of the special effect.  To designate the s
ource, click the "Select" button next to the "Source" field, and then click on the desired object in the editor's Main Window.
\par 
\par \tab }{\i\fs20 Dest. (Destination) ~
\par \tab }{\fs20 This is the destination of the special effe
ct.  Note, some special effects, smoke or fire, for example, do not have a destination.  This is intended more for spells or other effects that have a target.  To designate the destination, click the "Select" button next to the "Dest." field, and then cli
ck on the desired object in the editor's Main Window.
\par 
\par \tab }{\i\fs20 Stop All Scripts ~
\par \tab }{\fs20 This button will stop all scripts currently running in the editor.
\par 
\par \tab }{\i\fs20 Run Script ~
\par \tab }{\fs20 Once a source and destination, if needed, are set, clicking this button will run the script using the indicated source and destination to show the script's effect.
\par 
\par }{\i\fs20\ul Related Siege University Courses:
\par }{\fs20 301: Introduction to Dungeon Siege Architecture
\par 303: Skrit
\par }\pard \ql \li0\ri0\widctlpar\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0\itap0 {\f1\fs20 \page }{\cs19\f1\fs20\super #{\footnote \pard\plain \s18\ql \li0\ri0\widctlpar\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0\itap0 
\fs20\lang1033\langfe1033\cgrid\langnp1033\langfenp1033 {\cs19\super #}{ HIDD_DIALOG_STITCH_BUILDER}}}{\b\fs20\ul Stitch Builder:
\par }{\fs20 
\par Anytime new stitches are added to a map, the stitch builder must be used to incorporate the additions into the maps so that they will function in-game.
\par 
\par Selecting the map from the list and then clicking on the "Build" button will go activate the stitching process.
\par 
\par }{\i\fs20\ul Related Editor Dialogue Windows:
\par }{\fs20 Node ~> Stitch Manager
\par Tools ~> Auto-Stitch Tool
