\par }{\f1\fs20 \page }{\cs19\f1\fs20\super #{\footnote \pard\plain \s18\ql \li0\ri0\widctlpar\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0\itap0 \fs20\lang1033\langfe1033\cgrid\langnp1033\langfenp1033 {\cs19\super #}{ HIDD_DIALOG_PATH_MAKER}}}{\b\fs20\ul 
Tuning Path Editor:
\par 
\par }{\fs20 Tuning paths are a series of points that can be placed and then used to generate a variety of reports that can aid in balancing and tuning the pathed areas.
\par 
\par Prior to working with tuning paths, be sure that the View Tuning Points and Tuning Point Editing Mode options are active on their respective toolbars.  In each case, they are the buttons on the toolbar with a light blue exclamation point.
\par 
\par The three things that make up the visual parts of the tuning path in the Siege Editor Main Window are the tuning points (indicated by arrows), the path (a purple 
line conecting the points in the order they are placed), and the amount of space around each point that is included in any data collection (the yellow circle around each tuning point).
\par 
\par When reports are generated, data is gathered from the designated area around each tuning point.
\par 
\par }{\i\fs20\ul Current Path:
\par }{\fs20 This drop down menu displays the name of the currently selected path and allows the user to select other paths in the region.
\par 
\par }{\i\fs20\ul Point Sphere Properties:
\par }{\fs20 Using the settings here, the user can define the radius of the 
area around each tuning point from which data will be collected.  By doing so, the user controls how much of the area is included in any data generated.  Additionally, the user can set a tolerance level to include any objects that are "just outside" of th
e data collection area.
\par 
\par }{\i\fs20\ul Remove Path:
\par }{\fs20 Clicking on this button will delete the tuning path that is currently selected in the "Current Path" drop down menu.
\par 
\par }{\i\fs20\ul Mark as Duplicate:
\par }{\fs20 
Sometimes in order to include certain parts of a region in a tuning path, the path may sometimes need to double back on themselves.  In cases like this, tuning points can be marked as "Duplicates" to show that they duplicate the information area provided 
b
y another tuning point.  When marked as a "Duplicate", the data generated from a given tuning point will not be included in any reports generated.  To mark a tuning point as a Duplicate, select the point in the Main Window and then click on the "Mark as D
uplicate" button.  Duplicate tuning points will be indicated visually by turning a light shade of green.
\par 
\par }{\i\fs20\ul Mark as Elevator:
\par }{\fs20 If a tuning point is on an elevator, it can be marked as an "Elevator" tuning point so that generated reports are not skewed by dista
nce traveled by the elevator.  To mark a tuning point as an Elevator, select the point in the Main Window and then click on the "Mark as Elevator" button.  Elevator tuning points will be indicated visually by turning a light shade of purple. 
\par 
\par }{\i\fs20\ul Place Points:
\par }{\fs20 This button must be in its down position to place new tuning points.
\par 
\par }{\i\fs20\ul Reports:
\par }{\fs20 This button will open the "Tuning Path Reporter" dialogue window where the user selects which reports to produce and then generates them.
\par 
\par }{\i\fs20\ul Starting a New Tuning Path:
\par }{\fs20 In order to start a new tuning path, a tuning point must be laid down, then go into its object properties.  Approximately half way down the \'93Template Properties\'94 tab, under the heading of \'93dev_path_point\'94, the user can enter a \'93
path_name\'94.  After assigning a name to the path, the path will then be available in the \'93Current Path\'94 drop down menu on the tuning path editor dialogue box.
\par 
\par }{\i\fs20\ul Related Editor Dialogue Windows:
\par }{\fs20 View ~> Properties
\par Objects ~> Object Properties
\par Tools ~> Tuning Path Editor ~> Tuning Path Reporter
\par Tools ~> Batch Tuning Path Generator
\par 
