\b\fs20\ul Hide Component}{\b\i\fs20\ul :
\par 
\par }{\fs20 This tool allows the user to hide selected aspects of the Object Properties listings.  By either copying and pasting or simply typing the component name into the dialogue box and then clicking on the \'93Add\'94
 button, the indicated component will no longer show up in the object properties list.  The list of hidden properties is visible in the Hidden Components area and can be made viewable again by simply selecting them from the list and clicking on the \'93
Remove\'94 button.
\par 
\par NOTE ~ this is a global tool.  Any hidden components will be hidden for ALL objects.  Also be aware that when a component is hidden or unhidden, any open Object Properties must be closed and reopened before the change can be seen.
\par 
\par }{\i\fs20\ul Related Editor Dialogue Windows:}{\fs20 
\par Object ~> Object Properties
\par 
\par }{\i\fs20\ul Related Siege University Courses:
\par }{\fs20 102: Objects
\par 200: Dungeon Siege Concepts and Terminology}{\b\fs20\ul 
\par 
\par Command Editor:
\par 
\par }{\fs20 This tool is used to issue simple movement and patrol commands.  Once issued, if needed, the commands can be further edited in their respective prope
rties dialogue windows.  The commands are placed by selecting the actor, who will receive the command(s), then selecting the type of command to place, and then placing the command(s).
\par 
\par NOTE ~ by selecting the actor, then placing the commands, the commands 
will automatically be linked according to the order of their placement.  However, if edits are needed to add or remove commands, or if commands are placed without first selecting an actor, it is possible to link them together by using the Link button (see
 below).
\par 
\par }{\i\fs20\ul Tools:
\par }{\i\fs20 \tab Select ~
\par \tab }{\fs20 When this button is in the down position, the cursor can be used to select items without accidentally placing commands.
\par 
\par \tab }{\i\fs20 Link ~
\par }\pard \ql \fi-720\li720\ri0\widctlpar\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin720\itap0 {\i\fs20 \tab }{\fs20 Once a command is placed without a link to an actor or prior command, the link button can be used t
o link the desired objects together by selecting the object being linked FROM, then clicking the Link button, then selecting the object to be linked TO.
\par 
\par }{\i\fs20\ul Command:
\par }{\fs20 \tab }{\i\fs20 Move ~
\par \tab }{\fs20 
When this button is in the down position, the user can place movement commands by simply clicking the left mouse button.  In each case, the command placed is represented by a blue star and indicates the point to which the selected actor will move.   The p
l
acing of subsequent move commands will be linked to the previous command and will be the next point the actor travels to after reaching the prior command point.  There is no limit to the number of command points that can be assigned to an actor, but the m
ovement can be disturbed by other influences (attacking, defending, etc.).
\par 
\par \tab }{\i\fs20 Patrol ~
\par \tab }{\fs20 The patrol command is placed and functions in the same manner as the move command.  One major difference is that when patrol points are placed, the most recent command pl
aced automatically links to the first patrol command that was placed.  This forms a patrol pattern that will be looped continuously until the actor is broken out of the patrol.  This is unlike the movement commands, where the actor goes through the moveme
nt commands and stops at the last one.
\par }\pard \ql \li0\ri0\widctlpar\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0\itap0 {\fs20 
\par }{\i\fs20\ul Related Editor Dialogue Windows:
\par }{\fs20 View ~> Preferences
\par Object ~> Object Properties
