\par }\pard \ql \li0\ri0\widctlpar\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0\itap0 {\fs20 \page }{\cs19\fs20\super #{\footnote \pard\plain \s18\ql \li0\ri0\widctlpar\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0\itap0 
\fs20\lang1033\langfe1033\cgrid\langnp1033\langfenp1033 {\cs19\super #}{ HIDD_DIALOG_MOOD_EDITOR}}}{\b\fs20\ul Mood Editor:
\par }{\line This window is used to create and edit moods. Mood settings such as weather, fog, music, and general mood settings can be defined here \line \line }{\i\ul Global Mood List:}{ \line 
This window displays a list of all of the moods currently in the Dungeon Siege directory. \line \line }{\i\ul Tanked Moods:}{ \line This window displays a list of the moods available in a loaded tank version. NOTE: Moods listed in this w
indow can be tested, but CANNOT be edited. They must be converted to bits before editing is possible. \line \line }{\i\ul Create New Mood:}{ \line This button will add 
a new mood file to the folder selected in the Global Mood List. A prompt will pop up allowing the user to name the new mood file. NOTE: file names cannot contain spaces. If there is no folder in the Global Mood List, one must be added before a mood file c
an be added. \line \line }{\i\ul Add Mood Folder:}{ \line This button will add a new folder to the Global Mood List. A prompt will pop up allowing the user to name the new folder. \line \line }{\i\ul Delete Mood:}{ \line 
When clicked, this button will remove the currently selected file from the Global Mood List. \line \line }{\i\ul Edit Properties:}{ \line This button will take the user to the \'93Edit Mood Properties\'94
 window, where settings can be defined for transition time and frustum resizing. \line \line }{\i\ul Edit Weather:}{ \line This button will take the user to the \'93Edit Mood Weather\'94 window, where
 settings can be defined for rain, fog, wind, and interior. \line \line }{\i\ul Edit Fog:}{ \line This button will take the user to the \'93Edit Mood Fog\'94 window, where settings can be defined for fog color, density, and near/far distances. \line 
\line }{\i\ul Edit Music:}{ This button will take the user to the \'93Edit Mood Music\'94 window, where settings can be defined for ambient, standard, and battle music, as well as the room setting. \line \line }{\i\ul Test Mood:}{ \line 
This button will run the currently selected mood so that it can be viewed in the editor. \line \line }{\i\ul Stop Test:}{ \line This button will stop a mood test. \line \line }{\i\ul Music:}{ \line 
The three buttons in this area can only be used during a mood test. To test a mood's music, select the mood from the Global Mood List, click the \'93Test\'94 button, and then select any of the Test Ambient, Test Battle, o
r Test Standard buttons to listen to the music files currently associated with each of the respective mood music settings. \page }{\cs19\super #{\footnote \pard\plain \s18\ql \li0\ri0\widctlpar\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0\itap0 
\fs20\lang1033\langfe1033\cgrid\langnp1033\langfenp1033 {\cs19\super #}{ HIDD_DIALOG_MOOD_FOG}}}{\b\fs20\ul Edit Mood Fog:
\par 
\par }\pard\plain \s20\ql \li0\ri0\nowidctlpar\faauto\rin0\lin0\itap0 \f1\fs20\lang1033\langfe1033\cgrid\langnp1033\langfenp1033 {This window is used to edit settings for fog color, distances, and density in the selected mood.
\par }\pard\plain \ql \li0\ri0\widctlpar\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0\itap0 \fs24\lang1033\langfe1033\cgrid\langnp1033\langfenp1033 {\fs20 
\par }\pard\plain \s20\ql \li0\ri0\nowidctlpar\faauto\rin0\lin0\itap0 \f1\fs20\lang1033\langfe1033\cgrid\langnp1033\langfenp1033 {\i\ul Enable Fog:
\par }{This box must be checked to enable rain in the currently selected mood.
\par 
\par }\pard \s20\ql \fi-720\li720\ri0\nowidctlpar\faauto\rin0\lin720\itap0 {\i\ul Near Distance:
\par }\pard \s20\ql \li0\ri0\nowidctlpar\faauto\rin0\lin0\itap0 {This defines the distance from the camera (in meters) that the fog begins. You can still see past this point, usually up to the fog_far_dist, where the fog is 100% thick.
\par 
\par }{\i\ul Far Distance:
\par }{This is the distance from the camera (in meters) where the fog gets too thick to see through.
\par 
\par }{\i\ul Slow System Settings Near/Far Distance:
\par }{Alternative Near/Far Distance settings can be entered here. Typically, they are intended to
 be settings that are more subtle and easier on the system running the game. If, while running the game, Dungeon Siege detects that the system is a slower or is becoming \'93bogged down\'94, it will use these alternative settings.
\par }\pard\plain \ql \li0\ri0\widctlpar\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0\itap0 \fs24\lang1033\langfe1033\cgrid\langnp1033\langfenp1033 {\fs20 
\par }\pard\plain \s20\ql \fi-720\li720\ri0\nowidctlpar\faauto\rin0\lin720\itap0 \f1\fs20\lang1033\langfe1033\cgrid\langnp1033\langfenp1033 {\i\ul Fog Color:
\par }\pard \s20\ql \li0\ri0\nowidctlpar\faauto\rin0\lin0\itap0 {The small box of color shows the currently selected fog color, by clicking on the \'93Change\'94 button, the user will be taken to a color palette, where a new fog color can be selected.
\par }\pard \s20\ql \fi-720\li720\ri0\nowidctlpar\faauto\rin0\lin720\itap0 {
\par }{\i\ul Fog Density:
\par }\pard \s20\ql \li0\ri0\nowidctlpar\faauto\rin0\lin0\itap0 {This is a number from 0.0 to 1.0 that indicates how thick the fog is.  A setting of 0.0 represents no fog, while 1.0 is 100% fog.
\par }\pard\plain \ql \li0\ri0\widctlpar\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0\itap0 \fs24\lang1033\langfe1033\cgrid\langnp1033\langfenp1033 {\page }{\cs19\super #{\footnote \pard\plain 
\s18\ql \li0\ri0\widctlpar\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0\itap0 \fs20\lang1033\langfe1033\cgrid\langnp1033\langfenp1033 {\cs19\super #}{ HIDD_DIALOG_MOOD_MUSIC}}}{\b\fs20\ul Edit Mood Music:
\par 
\par }\pard\plain \s20\ql \li0\ri0\nowidctlpar\faauto\rin0\lin0\itap0 \f1\fs20\lang1033\langfe1033\cgrid\langnp1033\langfenp1033 {This window is used to edit settings for ambient, standard, and battle music selections in the current mood.
\par }\pard\plain \ql \li0\ri0\widctlpar\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0\itap0 \fs24\lang1033\langfe1033\cgrid\langnp1033\langfenp1033 {\fs20 
\par }\pard\plain \s20\ql \li0\ri0\nowidctlpar\faauto\rin0\lin0\itap0 \f1\fs20\lang1033\langfe1033\cgrid\langnp1033\langfenp1033 {\i\ul Ambient Track:
\par }{
\par \tab }{\i Sound File ~
\par }\pard \s20\ql \fi-720\li720\ri0\nowidctlpar\faauto\rin0\lin720\itap0 {\i \tab }{By clicking on the \'93\'85\'94
 button, a drop down menu will appear, from which the user can select which sound track will be used as ambient music. This is simply another track that will play at the same time as the music track. It is a great place to have a small looping track that
 plays creaking trees, or a gentle wind sound. The game doesn't really care what you put here - you could use this to play a second piece of music at the same time as the standard music track.
\par 
\par \tab }{\i Intro Delay ~
\par \tab }{This value is the number of seconds of silence
 to have before the track plays for the first time. If you want to use the ambient track to play a second piece of music, you can use this to stagger the two pieces of music, so that the ambient piece doesn't start until the standard piece is done.
\par 
\par \tab }{\i Repeat Delay ~
\par \tab }{This value is the number of seconds of silence to have after the end of the track. After this silence, the ambient track will play again. For any looping tracks, this should be 0.
\par 
\par }{\i\ul Standard Track:
\par 
\par }{\tab }{\i Sound File ~
\par \tab }{By clicking on the \'93\'85\'94 button, a drop down menu will appear, from which the user can select which sound track will be used as ambient music.  This is the standard track, usually used for music. As long as a file is located in sound\\
Music, it can be specified here. You don't need to provide the extension of the file.
\par 
\par \tab }{\i Intro Delay ~
\par \tab }{This value is the number of seconds of silence to have before the track plays for the first time. This can be useful if you are using a piece of music that starts with a sudden emphasis, but you are transition
ing into the mood for several seconds. Without an intro delay, the music would begin playing right away, but it would start out very quiet. Putting in a standard intro delay that is equal to (or longer than) the transition time will guarantee that the mus
ic will start at full volume.
\par 
\par \tab }{\i Repeat Delay ~
\par \tab }{This value is the number of seconds of silence to have after the end of the track. After this silence, the music will play again. For any looping tracks, this should be 0.
\par 
\par }{\i\ul Battle Track:
\par 
\par }\pard \s20\ql \li0\ri0\nowidctlpar\faauto\rin0\lin0\itap0 {The setting
s for Battle Tracks are the same as those mentioned above.  However, setting these values will have no effect unless you also modify mood_manager.skrit to actually play this track. This buried feature has a possible use for advanced modders.
\par 
\par The battle_tr
ack is a third music track that can be used, but it will take a little extra work to get it to work, and you will have to deal with skrit. It was originally added so that the standard track could play while the player was exploring, and then the battle tr
a
ck would play while the player was fighting. Fighting is so common, however, that it became an annoyance to have two different tracks of music constantly starting up and then changing. So the battle track became an unused feature. The mood_manager.skrit d
oesn't even play this track...but it could. It is located at world\\global\\moods\\mood_manager.skrit. 
\par 
\par }{\i\ul Room Type:
\par 
\par }{This is the EAX setting. EAX, when enabled on a soundcard that supports it, will filter many of the sounds in Dungeon Siege through reverb that
 you specify here. If you want to make it sound like the player is in a cave, set the room type to rt_cave. The default is rt_generic, which is a setting with no reverb.
\par 
\par }\pard\plain \ql \li0\ri0\widctlpar\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0\itap0 \fs24\lang1033\langfe1033\cgrid\langnp1033\langfenp1033 {\f1\fs20 \page }{\cs19\f1\fs20\super #{\footnote \pard\plain 
\s18\ql \li0\ri0\widctlpar\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0\itap0 \fs20\lang1033\langfe1033\cgrid\langnp1033\langfenp1033 {\cs19\super #}{ HIDD_DIALOG_MOOD_PROPERTIES}}}{\b\fs20\ul Edit Mood Properties:
\par 
\par }\pard\plain \s20\ql \li0\ri0\nowidctlpar\faauto\rin0\lin0\itap0 \f1\fs20\lang1033\langfe1033\cgrid\langnp1033\langfenp1033 {This window is used to edit basic settings for the functionality of the selected mood.
\par }\pard\plain \ql \li0\ri0\widctlpar\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0\itap0 \fs24\lang1033\langfe1033\cgrid\langnp1033\langfenp1033 {\fs20 
\par }{\i\fs20\ul General:
\par }\pard\plain \s20\ql \li0\ri0\nowidctlpar\faauto\rin0\lin0\itap0 \f1\fs20\lang1033\langfe1033\cgrid\langnp1033\langfenp1033 {
\par \tab }{\i Mood Name ~
\par \tab }{The name of the currently selected mood file.
\par 
\par \tab }{\i Transition Time ~
\par }\pard \s20\ql \fi-720\li720\ri0\nowidctlpar\faauto\rin0\lin720\itap0 {\i \tab }{The amount of time, in seconds, it will take a trigger to change the mood settings to those of the currently selected mood file.
\par 
\par \tab }{\i Interior (Indoor) Mood ~
\par \tab }{This is a true/false value that is used by the mood manager to control whether or not the fog will change color with the time of day. If interior = true, then the fog color will not change with the time of day.
\par }\pard\plain \ql \li0\ri0\widctlpar\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0\itap0 \fs24\lang1033\langfe1033\cgrid\langnp1033\langfenp1033 {\fs20 
\par }{\i\fs20\ul Frustum:
\par }\pard\plain \s20\ql \li0\ri0\nowidctlpar\faauto\rin0\lin0\itap0 \f1\fs20\lang1033\langfe1033\cgrid\langnp1033\langfenp1033 {
\par \tab }{\i Enable Frustum Resizing ~
\par \tab }{This box must be checked for the mood to be capable of resizing the players\rquote  frustum(s).
\par 
\par \tab }{\i Frustum Width ~
\par }\pard \s20\ql \fi-720\li720\ri0\nowidctlpar\faauto\rin0\lin720\itap0 {\i \tab }{Measurement in m
eters, recommended value is 45. We chose this number after carefully weighing world detail vs. system performance. This number is the radius of the frustum, where the player character is at the middle. That means that 45 meters out from the character in a
l
l directions, the world is loaded. If this number is changed to 55, you could push the fog out farther and you wouldn't see the edge of the world, because you have pushed back the edge of the world itself. The major downside of this is that you have drama
tically increased the number of things that the game has to keep in memory and actively process.
\par 
\par \tab }{\i Frustum Height ~
\par \tab }{Measurement is meters, default is 1000. We chose this number just because it was large enough that we wouldn't have to worry about it. This 
number was chosen before we came up with the crazy idea of really tall towers. When we came up with towers that would have bad guys on every floor, we suddenly had too many monsters in the frustum, so we changed the frustum height to 60. Even this is pret
ty large; it's very unlikely that you would ever notice in-game that the frustum isn't infinite in the vertical dimension.}{\ul 
\par }\pard\plain \ql \li0\ri0\widctlpar\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0\itap0 \fs24\lang1033\langfe1033\cgrid\langnp1033\langfenp1033 {\f1\fs20 \page }{\cs19\f1\fs20\super #{\footnote \pard\plain 
\s18\ql \li0\ri0\widctlpar\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0\itap0 \fs20\lang1033\langfe1033\cgrid\langnp1033\langfenp1033 {\cs19\super #}{ HIDD_DIALOG_MOOD_WEATHER}}}{\b\fs20\ul Edit Mood Weather:
\par 
\par }\pard\plain \s20\ql \li0\ri0\nowidctlpar\faauto\rin0\lin0\itap0 \f1\fs20\lang1033\langfe1033\cgrid\langnp1033\langfenp1033 {This window is used to edit settings for rain, wind, and snow levels in the selected mood.
\par }\pard\plain \ql \li0\ri0\widctlpar\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0\itap0 \fs24\lang1033\langfe1033\cgrid\langnp1033\langfenp1033 {\fs20 
\par }{\i\fs20\ul Rain:
\par }\pard\plain \s20\ql \li0\ri0\nowidctlpar\faauto\rin0\lin0\itap0 \f1\fs20\lang1033\langfe1033\cgrid\langnp1033\langfenp1033 {
\par \tab }{\i Enable Rain ~
\par \tab }{This box must be checked to enable rain in the currently selected mood.a
\par 
\par \tab }{\i Rain Density ~
\par }\pard \s20\ql \fi-720\li720\ri0\nowidctlpar\faauto\rin0\lin720\itap0 {\i \tab }{This is how thick the rain will be. Default is 0 (not raining). The maximum is defined in config\\weather.gas (we set it to 300).
\par 
\par \tab }{\i Lighting ~
\par \tab }{This can be set to either true or false. If true, then it uses settings in config\\weather.gas to determine how often the lightning will flash and how long after the flash the lightning occurs.
\par }\pard\plain \ql \li0\ri0\widctlpar\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0\itap0 \fs24\lang1033\langfe1033\cgrid\langnp1033\langfenp1033 {\fs20 
\par }{\i\fs20\ul Snow:
\par }\pard\plain \s20\ql \li0\ri0\nowidctlpar\faauto\rin0\lin0\itap0 \f1\fs20\lang1033\langfe1033\cgrid\langnp1033\langfenp1033 {
\par \tab }{\i Enable Snow ~
\par \tab }{This box must be checked to enable snow in the currently selected mood.
\par 
\par \tab }{\i Snow Density ~
\par }\pard \s20\ql \fi-720\li720\ri0\nowidctlpar\faauto\rin0\lin720\itap0 {\i \tab }{This is how thick the snow will be. Default is 0 (not snowing). The maximum is defined in config\\weather.gas (we set it to 400).
\par 
\par }{\i\ul Wind:
\par 
\par }\pard \s20\ql \li0\ri0\nowidctlpar\faauto\rin0\lin0\itap0 {\i \tab Enable Wind ~
\par }\pard \s20\ql \fi-720\li720\ri0\nowidctlpar\faauto\rin0\lin720\itap0 {\i \tab }{This box must be checked to enable wind in the currently selected mood.}{\i 
\par }{
\par \tab }{\i Wind Velocity ~
\par \tab }{Velocity of the wind, in meters per second. Default is 0. If you set this above 5, you may see very strange things happen with effects that are not wind immune, if y
ou make any. In tests, monsters that breathed non-wind-immune fire had a lot of trouble hitting players right in front of them when the wind was set to 20+. We tend to leave the wind at 5 or less.
\par 
\par \tab }{\i Wind Direction ~
\par \tab }{Direction of the wind, specified in radians angles from the north vector. So pi/2 would be east, pi would be south, etc.}{\i\ul 
\par }\pard\plain \ql \li0\ri0\nowidctlpar\faauto\rin0\lin0\itap0 \fs24\lang1033\langfe1033\cgrid\langnp1033\langfenp1033 {\f1\fs20 
\par }}